@macro vmdocvmvisitwhensaving
@defopt vm-visit-when-saving
@vindex vm-visit-when-saving
Value determines whether VM will visit folders when saving messages.
@code{Visiting} means that VM will read the folder into Emacs and append the
message to the buffer instead of appending to the folder file directly.
This behavior is ideal when folders are encrypted or compressed since
appending plaintext directly to such folders is a ghastly mistake.

A value of t means VM will always visit folders when saving.

A nil value means VM will never visit folders before saving to them, and
VM will generate an error if you attempt to save messages to a folder
that is being visited.  The latter restriction is necessary to insure
that the buffer and disk copies of the folder being visited remain
consistent.

A value other than nil or t means that VM will save to the folder
buffer if it is visited or to the file otherwise.

Default value: @code{not-always}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmdiscardcacheddata
@deffn Command vm-discard-cached-data &optional count
@findex vm-discard-cached-data
Discard cached information about the current message.
When VM gathers information from the headers of a message, it stores it
internally for future reference.  This command causes VM to forget this
information, and VM will be forced to search the headers of the message
again for these data.  VM will also have to decide again which headers
should be displayed and which should not.  Therefore this command is
useful if you change the value of vm-visible-headers or
vm-invisible-header-regexp in the midst of a VM session.

Numeric prefix argument N means to discard data from the current message
plus the next N-1 messages.  A negative N means discard data from the
current message and the previous N-1 messages.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
data is discarded only from the marked messages in the current folder.
If applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread operations are
enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the
thread have their cached data discarded.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmeditmessage
@deffn Command vm-edit-message &optional prefix-argument
@findex vm-edit-message
Edit the current message.  Prefix arg means mark as unedited instead.
If editing, the current message is copied into a temporary buffer, and
this buffer is selected for editing.  The major mode of this buffer is
controlled by the variable vm-edit-message-mode.  The hooks specified
in vm-edit-message-hook are run just prior to returning control to the user
for editing.

Use C-c ESC when you have finished editing the message.  The message
will be inserted into its folder replacing the old version of the
message.  If you don’t want your edited version of the message to
replace the original, use C-c C-] and the edit will be aborted.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmeditmessageabort
@deffn Command vm-edit-message-abort
@findex vm-edit-message-abort
Abort the edit of a message, forgetting changes to the message.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmeditmessageend
@deffn Command vm-edit-message-end
@findex vm-edit-message-end
End the edit of a message and copy the result to its folder.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmeditmessageotherframe
@deffn Command vm-edit-message-other-frame &optional prefix
@findex vm-edit-message-other-frame
Like vm-edit-message, but run in a newly created frame.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgaskhook
@deffn Command vm-epg-ask-hook
@findex vm-epg-ask-hook
Ask whether to sign or encrypt outgoing messages with PGP/MIME.

Add to @code{vm-mail-send-hook} to be asked each time you send a message.
@code{vm-epg-ask-function} controls what is asked: by default
@code{vm-epg-prompt-for-action} offers a choice of actions, but it can also name
a single action to confirm, or your own function.

This hook must be last in @code{vm-mail-send-hook}, and signals an error if it is
not: signing covers the message as it stands, so a later hook that modified
the message would invalidate the signature.  Add it with the APPEND argument
to @code{add-hook}:

       (add-hook 'vm-mail-send-hook #'vm-epg-ask-hook t)
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgattachpublickey
@deffn Command vm-epg-attach-public-key
@findex vm-epg-attach-public-key
Attach a public key to the composition as an application/pgp-keys part.
The key exported is the author’s, taken from the headers listed in
@code{vm-epg-get-author-headers}.  When that yields no address, or the variable
is nil, prompt for a user ID -- so any key in your keyring can be sent, not
only your own.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgcleartextdecrypt
@deffn Command vm-epg-cleartext-decrypt
@findex vm-epg-cleartext-decrypt
Decrypt the inline PGP message in the current message.
Replace the ASCII armor in a presentation copy with the plaintext, leaving
the folder unmodified, and report the outcome in the modeline.  A decryption
failure inserts the error text instead, faced with @code{vm-epg-error}.

If the plaintext is itself an inline signed message, verify it as well with
@code{vm-epg-cleartext-verify}.

Refuses to run on a read-only folder even though only the presentation copy
is written, unlike @code{vm-epg-cleartext-verify}, which does not check.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgcleartextencrypt
@deffn Command vm-epg-cleartext-encrypt sign
@findex vm-epg-cleartext-encrypt
Encrypt the composition body in place as inline PGP ASCII armor.
With a prefix argument, SIGN non-nil, sign it as well.

This replaces the body with the armor rather than building a MIME structure
around it, so it cannot cover attachments; prefer @code{vm-epg-encrypt}, which
produces PGP/MIME.  Also used internally by @code{vm-epg-encrypt} to armor the
body it then wraps.

Every recipient must have a usable encryption key: with no recipient key
this signals an error rather than falling back to symmetric (passphrase)
encryption, which is never what is wanted for mail.  As with
@code{vm-epg-encrypt}, your own key is not added; an encrypt-to line in gpg.conf
is what adds it.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgcleartextsign
@deffn Command vm-epg-cleartext-sign
@findex vm-epg-cleartext-sign
Sign the composition body in place as inline PGP ASCII armor.
This uses the OpenPGP cleartext signature framework, which leaves the text
readable and appends the signature to the body, rather than building a
multipart/signed MIME structure around it.  It therefore cannot cover
attachments; prefer @code{vm-epg-sign}, which produces PGP/MIME.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgcleartextverify
@deffn Command vm-epg-cleartext-verify
@findex vm-epg-cleartext-verify
Verify the inline PGP signature in the current message.
Replace the ASCII armor in a presentation copy of the message with a
description of the signature, faced according to whether it verified, and
report the outcome in the modeline.  The folder itself is not modified.

If the signing key is not in your keyring and @code{vm-epg-fetch-missing-keys}
is non-nil, try to fetch it from a keyserver first.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgcomposemode
@deffn Command vm-epg-compose-mode &optional arg
@findex vm-epg-compose-mode
Minor mode for composing PGP/MIME messages with EPG.

Toggle the mode when ARG is nil, enable it when ARG is a positive number
and disable it otherwise.  Enabling makes the bindings below and the
PGP/MIME menu available in the composition buffer.

@example
Key             Binding
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
C-c # C-e       vm-epg-cleartext-encrypt
C-c # C-s       vm-epg-cleartext-sign
C-c # E         vm-epg-sign-and-encrypt
C-c # a         vm-epg-ask-hook
C-c # e         vm-epg-encrypt
C-c # k         vm-epg-attach-public-key
C-c # s         vm-epg-sign
@end example
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgencrypt
@deffn Command vm-epg-encrypt &optional sign
@findex vm-epg-encrypt
Encrypt the composition as PGP/MIME, a multipart/encrypted message.
With a prefix argument, SIGN non-nil, sign it as well, which is what
@code{vm-epg-sign-and-encrypt} does.

Every recipient address found in the headers listed in
@code{vm-epg-get-recipients-headers} must have a usable encryption key in your
keyring; otherwise this signals an error and leaves the composition
untouched.  Note that the message is never encrypted to a passphrase: if no
recipient key can be found, it refuses rather than falling back to symmetric
encryption.

Encryption is to those recipients and to nobody else, so a copy filed with
FCC: is one you cannot read back.  To encrypt to yourself as well, put a
line reading encrypt-to followed by your key id in ~/.gnupg/gpg.conf.  VM
passes GnuPG no --no-encrypt-to, so that setting is honoured.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepginsertpublickey
@deffn Command vm-epg-insert-public-key
@findex vm-epg-insert-public-key
Insert a public key as ASCII armor into the composition at point.
The key is selected as for @code{vm-epg-attach-public-key}, but is inserted
inline rather than attached as a MIME part.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgsign
@deffn Command vm-epg-sign
@findex vm-epg-sign
Sign the composition with PGP/MIME, as a multipart/signed message.

RFC 3156 forbids 8bit content transfer encoding in signed messages, and
lines beginning with "From " must be armored, because a mail gateway that
re-encodes either one would invalidate the signature.

If the composition is not yet MIME-encoded, this encodes it in a
signature-safe way, using @code{vm-epg-sign-text-transfer-encoding} in place of
@code{vm-mime-8bit-text-transfer-encoding} and forcing
@code{vm-mime-composition-armor-from-lines} on; your normal settings for those
two variables therefore do not matter here.

If the composition has *already* been MIME-encoded -- for instance because
you encoded it yourself with @code{vm-mime-encode-composition} -- this cannot
re-encode it safely, so it checks for the two hazards above and refuses to
sign rather than produce a signature that breaks in transit.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgsignandencrypt
@deffn Command vm-epg-sign-and-encrypt
@findex vm-epg-sign-and-encrypt
Sign and encrypt the composition as PGP/MIME.
Equivalent to @code{vm-epg-encrypt} with a prefix argument.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgsnarfkeys
@deffn Command vm-epg-snarf-keys
@findex vm-epg-snarf-keys
Import into your keyring the public keys in the body of the current message.
This treats the whole message body as key material, so it is meant for
messages that are an inline PGP public key block.  Keys arriving as an
application/pgp-keys MIME part are handled by
@code{vm-mime-display-internal-application/pgp-keys} instead.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgcomposemodemenu
@deffn Command vm-epg-compose-mode-menu arg1
@findex vm-epg-compose-mode-menu
PGP/MIME compose mode menu.

@emph{Not autoloaded}: VM has to be loaded before @kbd{M-x} offers this one.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgpromptforaction
@deffn Command vm-epg-prompt-for-action
@findex vm-epg-prompt-for-action
Prompt for a PGP action and return it.
The choices come from @code{vm-epg-prompt-action-alist}; RET repeats the previous
choice, and @code{q} aborts sending with an error.

@emph{Not autoloaded}: VM has to be loaded before @kbd{M-x} offers this one.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgaskfunction
@defopt vm-epg-ask-function
@vindex vm-epg-ask-function
What @code{vm-epg-ask-hook} should do before sending a message.

The value is either an action symbol or a function:

@example
  nil                do nothing;
  ‘sign’             ask whether to sign;
  ‘encrypt’          ask whether to encrypt;
  ‘sign-and-encrypt’ ask whether to sign and encrypt;
  a function         called with no arguments, returning one of the action
                     symbols above, or nil for no action.

@end example
An action symbol ACTION selects the command @code{vm-epg-ACTION}, so any value
other than those listed must name an existing @code{vm-epg-} command.

Default value: @code{vm-epg-prompt-for-action}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgautodecrypt
@defopt vm-epg-auto-decrypt
@vindex vm-epg-auto-decrypt
If non-nil, decrypt encrypted messages automatically when displaying them.
When nil, a button is shown instead and decryption happens only when you
activate it.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgautosnarf
@defopt vm-epg-auto-snarf
@vindex vm-epg-auto-snarf
If non-nil, snarf public keys automatically.
Snarfing means importing the public keys found in a message into your GnuPG
keyring.  When nil, a button is shown instead and keys are imported only
when you activate it.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgcleartextsearchlimit
@defopt vm-epg-cleartext-search-limit
@vindex vm-epg-cleartext-search-limit
Number of bytes to search into the message for a PGP clear text armor.

Default value: @code{4096}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgfetchmissingkeys
@defopt vm-epg-fetch-missing-keys
@vindex vm-epg-fetch-missing-keys
If non-nil, fetch missing keys from a keyserver when verifying signatures.
When a signature was made by a key that is not in your keyring, contact the
keyserver configured for GnuPG to retrieve it and then verify again.  This
makes displaying a signed message reach out to the network.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepggetauthorheaders
@defopt vm-epg-get-author-headers
@vindex vm-epg-get-author-headers
The list of headers used to identify the author of an outgoing message.
The first address found in these headers is used to select the signing key.
If nil, the default EPG signing key is used.

Default value: @code{("From:" "Sender:")}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepggetrecipientsheaders
@defopt vm-epg-get-recipients-headers
@vindex vm-epg-get-recipients-headers
The list of headers used to identify the recipients of an outgoing message.
Every address found in these headers must have a usable encryption key, or
encryption fails.

Default value: @code{("To:" "CC:" "BCC:")}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmepgsigntexttransferencoding
@defopt vm-epg-sign-text-transfer-encoding
@vindex vm-epg-sign-text-transfer-encoding
The content transfer encoding used for signed MIME parts of type text.

RFC 3156 forbids 8bit encoding in signed messages, because a gateway that
re-encodes the body would invalidate the signature.  @code{vm-epg-sign}
therefore binds @code{vm-mime-8bit-text-transfer-encoding} to this value while
it encodes the composition, overriding your normal setting for the duration
of the signing operation.

Both choices are signature-safe; @code{quoted-printable} keeps mostly-ASCII text
readable to humans and to non-MIME tools, while @code{base64} is more compact for
text that is largely non-ASCII.

Default value: @code{quoted-printable}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmenableexternalmessages
@defopt vm-enable-external-messages
@vindex vm-enable-external-messages
Non-nil value should be a list of contexts in which VM may
use message bodies stored externally.  External messages are
those stored in external sources such as the file system or
remote mail servers.  In some cases, VM is able to work with
minimal header information of the messages, without loading the
entire message bodies into the folder buffers.

This allows faster start-up times and smaller memory images of
Emacs sessions, at the cost of short delays when messages are
viewed.

As of version 8.2.0, this facility is only available for IMAP
folders (context name @code{imap}).  Messages larger than
@code{vm-imap-max-message-size} are treated as external messages.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmexternalfetchmessageforpresentation
@defopt vm-external-fetch-message-for-presentation
@vindex vm-external-fetch-message-for-presentation
Non-nil value indicates that external message bodies should be
automatically fetched for message presentation.  If it is nil, then
messages will be presented with only the headers and @code{vm-load-message}
must be used to load the externally stored message bodies.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmexternalfetchedmessagelimit
@defopt vm-external-fetched-message-limit
@vindex vm-external-fetched-message-limit
Should be an integer representing the maximum number of messages
that VM should keep in the Folder buffer when the messages are
fetched on demand, or nil to signify no limit.

Default value: @code{10}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmattachmentbuttonface
@defopt vm-attachment-button-face
@vindex vm-attachment-button-face
Face used for text in buttons that trigger the display of MIME objects.

Default value: @code{vm-attachment-button}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmattachmentbuttonmouseface
@defopt vm-attachment-button-mouse-face
@vindex vm-attachment-button-mouse-face
Face used for text in MIME buttons when mouse is hovering.

Default value: @code{vm-attachment-button-mouse}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcitationfaces
@defopt vm-citation-faces
@vindex vm-citation-faces
Faces for quoted text, one per level of quoting.
The first is for text quoted once, the second for text quoted twice, and so
on.  Text quoted deeper than there are faces here wears the last of them, so
a list of one face colours every level alike and a list of none turns
citation colouring off while leaving the signature face alone.

Default value:
@example
(vm-citation-1 vm-citation-2 vm-citation-3 vm-citation-4 vm-citation-5)
@end example
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmenablebodyfaces
@defopt vm-enable-body-faces
@vindex vm-enable-body-faces
Non-nil colours quoted text and the signature in a message body.

Quoted text wears the faces of @code{vm-citation-faces}, one per level, and the
signature wears @code{vm-signature-face}.  Headers are a separate matter, decided
by @code{vm-highlighted-header-regexp}.

Set this in the init file.  Off by default because it changes how every
message looks.  In earlier releases the same colouring came from the bundled
u-vm-color add-on, which had to be wired up by hand and is gone
(emacs-vm/vm#811).
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhighlighturlface
@defopt vm-highlight-url-face
@vindex vm-highlight-url-face
Non-nil value should be a face to use display URLs found in messages.
Nil means don’t highlight URLs.

Default value: @code{vm-highlight-url}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhighlightedheaderface
@defopt vm-highlighted-header-face
@vindex vm-highlighted-header-face
Face to be used to highlight headers.
The headers to highlight are specified by the @code{vm-highlighted-header-regexp}
variable.

Default value: @code{vm-highlighted-header}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmimebuttonface
@defopt vm-mime-button-face
@vindex vm-mime-button-face
Face used for text in buttons that trigger the display of MIME objects.

Default value: @code{vm-mime-button}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmimebuttonmouseface
@defopt vm-mime-button-mouse-face
@vindex vm-mime-button-mouse-face
Face used for text in MIME buttons when mouse is hovering.

Default value: @code{vm-mime-button-mouse}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsignatureface
@defopt vm-signature-face
@vindex vm-signature-face
Face for the signature at the end of a message body.
The signature is what follows a line of exactly "-- ", the separator
RFC 3676 describes, which is what mail readers write.  Nil colours it not at
all.

Default value: @code{vm-signature}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsummaryenablefaces
@defopt vm-summary-enable-faces
@vindex vm-summary-enable-faces
A non-NIL value enables the use of faces in the summary buffer.

You should set this variable in the init-file.  For interactive use,
the command @code{vm-summary-faces-mode} should be used.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsummaryhighlightface
@defopt vm-summary-highlight-face
@vindex vm-summary-highlight-face
Face to use to highlight the summary entry for the current message.
Nil means don’t highlight the current message’s summary entry.

Default value: @code{vm-summary-highlight}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmbackupfolder
@deffn Command vm-backup-folder
@findex vm-backup-folder
Keep a copy of this folder as it is on disk.
Emacs backs a file up on the first save of its buffer and not again, so a
folder saved earlier in this session has no copy of what is on disk now.
This makes one whatever @code{make-backup-files} says.

The copy is named as Emacs would name a backup: @code{backup-directory-alist}
decides where it goes and the numbered-backup settings how many are kept, so
it lands where your other backups are.

It copies the file and not the buffer, so changes you have not saved are not
in it.  Save the folder first to keep those.

Run it from anywhere in a folder, the summary included.  @code{backup-buffer}
does nothing in a summary or presentation buffer, those visiting no file,
which is what makes a command of VM’s own worth having.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmchangefoldertype
@deffn Command vm-change-folder-type type &optional file output
@findex vm-change-folder-type
Change folder type to TYPE.
The old name @code{From_-with-Content-Length} is accepted for @code{mboxcl2}.
TYPE may be one of the following symbol values:

@example
    From_
    mboxcl2
    BellFrom_
    mmdf
    babyl

@end example
Interactively TYPE will be read from the minibuffer.

With a prefix argument, or with FILE given, convert a folder on disk that VM
is not visiting.  That is how to repair a folder VM will not read -- see
@code{vm-change-folder-type-of-file}.

With two prefix arguments, or with OUTPUT given, the converted folder is
written to a file of your naming and the one converted is left as it was.
OUTPUT wants FILE: to convert the folder you are in into a new file, save it
and convert that.  A name that cannot hold TYPE is refused rather than
written, here and on disk both -- a folder called out.mbox cannot hold
mboxcl2 (emacs-vm/vm#763).

The folder’s current type is offered as well as the others: converting a
folder to what it already is rewrites every message in it, which for mboxcl2
recomputes every @code{Content-Length}.  That is the repair for a folder whose
lengths are wrong -- and a wrong length is not a missing one, so such a folder
opens without complaint and the reader quietly falls back on searching for the
next separator.

Without FILE the folder in the current buffer is converted, and a buffer whose
visit failed partway is refused: it holds only the messages read before the
error, and the on-disk conversion is what such a folder wants.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcheckfolder
@deffn Command vm-check-folder &optional file
@findex vm-check-folder
Report this folder’s type and check that it is sound, writing nothing.

With a prefix argument, or with FILE given, check a folder on disk that VM is
not visiting -- see @code{vm-check-folder-of-file}.  That is how to check a folder
VM will not read, which is the folder most likely to want it.

Says what type the folder is, what its name says it is, what its contents say
and what the default is, how many messages it holds against how many the
reader finds by walking the separators, and for an mboxcl2 folder whether every
message’s @code{Content-Length} matches its body.

What the contents say is counted over every message, and the name is not
consulted for it: the reader takes the type from the name, so a folder
carrying a length on every message under a name that does not say mboxcl2 is
read as From_ and split wherever a body line begins "From ".  Nothing else
tells the reader that, and it is the question the warning at visit time
leaves open.

A wrong length is the fault this is for, because it is the one that gives no
other sign.  A missing one is refused when the folder is visited, but a
length that is merely wrong opens without complaint: the reader falls back on
searching for the next separator when the count does not land on one, so VM
reads the folder correctly while anything that believes the header -- which is
what the format is for -- takes the wrong bytes.

A POP or IMAP cache whose name states no type is reported too, and
@code{vm-convert-caches-to-mboxcl2} named as what converts it.  Nothing in such a
cache is wrong, so this is the one place a reader is told: it is read as From_,
where a message whose body holds a line beginning "From " can split in two.

Nothing is written.  @code{vm-change-folder-type} is the repair: converting a
folder to the type it already is recomputes every length.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmconvertcachestomboxcl2
@deffn Command vm-convert-caches-to-mboxcl2 &optional each
@findex vm-convert-caches-to-mboxcl2
Convert every POP and IMAP cache whose name does not state its type.
A cache VM creates now is named for its type and written as mboxcl2, where the
end of a message is a byte count rather than a line that has to be recognised.
A cache from before that has no such name and is read as From_, so a message
whose body holds a line beginning "From " can still split it in two.  This
converts each of those and renames it, which is @code{vm-change-folder-type-of-file}
once per cache: the previous contents are kept in a backup file, and a cache
that is already mboxcl2 in all but its name is only renamed.

It asks once before starting, and then once per cache about deleting the copy
left under the old name -- that one is a file on disk and a decision of its
own, so it is asked rather than assumed either way.  With a prefix argument it
asks about each cache before converting it as well.

A cache being visited cannot be converted and is reported; quit that folder
with @code{vm-quit} and run this again.  Nothing is refetched, and a cache that
cannot be read is left exactly as it was.  Where anything could not be
converted the faults are listed in a buffer, since a run of them in the echo
area cannot be read.

The caches are looked for where their names are built, which is
@code{vm-imap-folder-cache-directory}, @code{vm-pop-folder-cache-directory},
@code{vm-folder-directory} and the home directory.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmemittotalsblurb
@deffn Command vm-emit-totals-blurb
@findex vm-emit-totals-blurb
Show the line @code{vm-totals-blurb} answers, and answer with it.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmfoldercachefile
@deffn Command vm-folder-cache-file &optional buffer
@findex vm-folder-cache-file
Say which file holds the local cache of this POP or IMAP folder.

Answers nil for a folder that is a file in the first place.  BUFFER is the
folder to ask about, the current one by default -- and a summary or
presentation buffer counts as its folder, since that is where the reader
is when the question occurs to them.  In a virtual folder the answer is
about the folder the message being looked at really lives in.

A cache file is named after the MD5 of the maildrop, so it can be neither
read nor typed by hand.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmfoldername
@deffn Command vm-folder-name
@findex vm-folder-name
Return the current folder’s name (local file name, or POP/IMAP
maildrop string).
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmgetnewmail
@deffn Command vm-get-new-mail &optional arg
@findex vm-get-new-mail
Move any new mail that has arrived in any of the spool files for the
current folder into the folder.  New mail is appended to the disk
and buffer copies of the folder.

Prefix arg means to gather mail from a user specified folder, instead of
the usual spool files.  The file name will be read from the minibuffer.
Unlike when getting mail from a spool file, the source file is left
undisturbed after its messages have been copied.

Two prefix args (C-u C-u) mean to fetch everything an IMAP mailbox
has and this folder has not, including the messages @code{vm-imap-retrieved-messages}
records as fetched once already.  That record is what stops a message deleted
here on purpose from coming back, so this is not the way to read mail day to
day; it is how to refill a folder whose cache lost messages the record still
names.  It gathers from no other folder, and other access methods ignore it.

When applied to a virtual folder, this command runs itself on
each of the underlying real folders associated with this virtual
folder.  A prefix argument has no effect when this command is
applied to virtual folder; mail is always gathered from the spool
files.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhelp
@deffn Command vm-help
@findex vm-help
Display help for various VM activities.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmloadmessage
@deffn Command vm-load-message &optional count
@findex vm-load-message
Load the message by retrieving its body from its
permanent location.  Currently this facility is only available for IMAP
folders.

With a prefix argument COUNT, the current message and the next 
COUNT - 1 messages are loaded.  A negative argument means
the current message and the previous |COUNT| - 1 messages are
loaded.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
only marked messages are loaded, other messages are ignored.  If
applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread operations are
enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the
thread are loaded.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmarkmessageread
@deffn Command vm-mark-message-read &optional count
@findex vm-mark-message-read
Mark the current message as read, i.e., set the @code{unread} and @code{new}
attributes to nil.  If the message is already marked as read, then
it is left unchanged.

Numeric prefix argument N means to unread the current message plus the
next N-1 messages.  A negative N means mark the current message and
the previous N-1 messages as read.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
all marked messages are affected, other messages are ignored.  If
applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread operations are
enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the
thread are affected.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmarkmessageunread
@deffn Command vm-mark-message-unread &optional count
@findex vm-mark-message-unread
Mark the current message as unread.  If the message is already
new or unread, then it is left unchanged.

Numeric prefix argument N means to mark the current message plus
the next N-1 messages as unread.  A negative N means mark the
current message and the previous N-1 messages as unread.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
all marked messages are affected, other messages are ignored.  If
applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread operations are
enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the
thread are affected.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmquit
@deffn Command vm-quit &optional no-expunge no-change
@findex vm-quit
Quit visiting the current folder, saving changes.  If the folder is
being visited read-only then changes are not saved.  This behavior
can be customized using @code{vm-preserve-read-only-folders-on-disk}.

If the customization variable @code{vm-expunge-before-quit} is set to
  non-nil value then deleted messages are expunged.

Giving a prefix argument overrides the variable and no expunge is
done.

When called internally, the optional argument NO-EXPUNGE says
that the deleted messages should not be expunged (irrespective of
the value of @code{vm-expunge-before-quit}.  NO-CHANGE says that
changes should be discarded.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmquitjustbury
@deffn Command vm-quit-just-bury
@findex vm-quit-just-bury
Bury the current VM folder and its auxiliary buffers.
The folder is not altered and Emacs is still visiting it.  You
can switch back to it with switch-to-buffer or by using the
Buffer Menu.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmquitjusticonify
@deffn Command vm-quit-just-iconify
@findex vm-quit-just-iconify
Iconify the frame and bury the current VM folder and summary buffers.
The folder is not altered and Emacs is still visiting it.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmquitnochange
@deffn Command vm-quit-no-change
@findex vm-quit-no-change
Quit visiting the current folder and discard any changes made to the folder.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmquitnoexpunge
@deffn Command vm-quit-no-expunge
@findex vm-quit-no-expunge
Quit visiting the current folder without expunging deleted
messages.  

The setting of @code{vm-expunge-before-quit} is ignored.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmrecoverfile
@deffn Command vm-recover-file
@findex vm-recover-file
Recover the autosave file for the current folder.
Same as M-x vm-recover-folder.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmrecoverfolder
@deffn Command vm-recover-folder
@findex vm-recover-folder
Recover the autosave file for the current folder.
Same as M-x vm-recover-folder.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmrefreshmessage
@deffn Command vm-refresh-message
@findex vm-refresh-message
Reload the message body from its permanent location.  Currently
this facility is only available for IMAP folders.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmreordermessageheaders
@deffn Command vm-reorder-message-headers message &rest rest
@findex vm-reorder-message-headers

@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmrevertbuffer
@deffn Command vm-revert-buffer
@findex vm-revert-buffer
Revert the current folder to its version on the disk.
The summary and presentation buffers are killed, the file is read again,
and the folder is visited afresh with the access method it had, so an
IMAP or POP folder comes back connected rather than as a plain file.

Also available as @code{vm-revert-folder}.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmrevertfolder
@deffn Command vm-revert-folder
@findex vm-revert-folder
Revert the current folder to its version on the disk.
The summary and presentation buffers are killed, the file is read again,
and the folder is visited afresh with the access method it had, so an
IMAP or POP folder comes back connected rather than as a plain file.

Also available as @code{vm-revert-folder}.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsaveandexpungefolder
@deffn Command vm-save-and-expunge-folder &optional prefix
@findex vm-save-and-expunge-folder
Expunge folder, then save it to disk.
Prefix arg is handled the same as for the command @code{save-buffer}.
Expunge won’t be done if folder is read-only.

When applied to a virtual folder, this command works as if you had
run @code{vm-expunge-folder} followed by @code{vm-save-folder}.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsavebuffer
@deffn Command vm-save-buffer prefix
@findex vm-save-buffer
Not documented.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsavefolder
@deffn Command vm-save-folder &optional prefix
@findex vm-save-folder
Save current folder to disk.
Prefix arg is handled the same as for the command @code{save-buffer}.

If the customization variable @code{vm-expunge-before-save} is set to
non-nil value then deleted messages are expunged.

When applied to a virtual folder, this command runs itself on
each of the underlying real folders associated with the virtual
folder.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsavefoldernoexpunge
@deffn Command vm-save-folder-no-expunge &optional prefix
@findex vm-save-folder-no-expunge
Save current folder to disk.
Prefix arg is handled the same as for the command @code{save-buffer}.  

Deleted messages are _not_ expunged irrespective of the variable
@code{vm-expunge-before-save}.

When applied to a virtual folder, this command runs itself on
each of the underlying real folders associated with the virtual
folder.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmtogglereadonly
@deffn Command vm-toggle-read-only
@findex vm-toggle-read-only
If the current VM folder is read-only, make it modifiable.

This command can also be used to make a modifiable folder read-only.
However it is unsafe to do so because any previous modifications will
be discarded when the folder is quit.  You should first save the
current changes of the folder before making it read-only.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmunloadmessage
@deffn Command vm-unload-message &optional count physical
@findex vm-unload-message
Unload the message body, i.e., delete it from the folder
buffer.  It can be retrieved again in future from its permanent
external location.  Currently this facility is only available for
IMAP folders.

With a prefix argument COUNT, the current message and the next 
COUNT - 1 messages are unloaded.  A negative argument means
the current message and the previous |COUNT| - 1 messages are
unloaded.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}), only 
marked messages are unloaded, other messages are ignored.  If
applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread operations are
enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in
the thread are unloaded.

If the optional argument PHYSICAL is non-nil, then the message is
physically discarded.  Otherwise, the discarding may be delayed until
the folder is saved.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmunreadmessage
@deffn Command vm-unread-message &optional count
@findex vm-unread-message
Mark the current message as unread.  If the message is already
new or unread, then it is left unchanged.

Numeric prefix argument N means to mark the current message plus
the next N-1 messages as unread.  A negative N means mark the
current message and the previous N-1 messages as unread.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
all marked messages are affected, other messages are ignored.  If
applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread operations are
enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the
thread are affected.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmwritefile
@deffn Command vm-write-file
@findex vm-write-file
Write this folder to a file of another name, as @code{write-file} does.

Three things @code{write-file} does not do.  The file is created with
@code{vm-default-folder-permission-bits}, so a folder does not become
world-readable through being written somewhere new.  The message totals
are stored against the new name for the folders summary, so it does not
have to open the folder to know them.  And the summary and presentation
buffers are renamed to follow the folder.

Refuses on a virtual folder, which has no file of its own.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmautogetnewmail
@defopt vm-auto-get-new-mail
@vindex vm-auto-get-new-mail
Non-nil value causes VM to automatically move mail from spool files
to a mail folder when the folder is first visited.  Nil means
you must always use @code{vm-get-new-mail} to pull in newly arrived messages.

If the value is a number, then it specifies how often (in
seconds) VM should check for new mail and try to retrieve it.
This is done asynchronously using a timer task and may occur
while you are editing other files.  It should not disturb your
editing, except perhaps for a pause while the check is being
done.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmberkeleymailcompatibility
@defopt vm-berkeley-mail-compatibility
@vindex vm-berkeley-mail-compatibility
Non-nil means to read and write BSD Mail(1) style Status: headers.
This makes sense if you plan to use VM to read mail archives created by
Mail.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcheckfoldertypes
@defopt vm-check-folder-types
@vindex vm-check-folder-types
Non-nil value causes VM to check folder and message types for
compatibility before it performs certain operations.

Before saving a message to a folder, VM will check that the destination folder
is of the same type as the message to be saved.

Before incorporating message into a visited folder, VM will check that the
messages are of the same type as that folder.

A nil value means don’t do the checks.

If non-nil, VM will either convert the messages to the appropriate
type before saving or incorporating them, or it will signal an
error.  The value of @code{vm-convert-folder-types} determines which
action VM will take.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmconfirmnewfolders
@defopt vm-confirm-new-folders
@vindex vm-confirm-new-folders
Non-nil value causes interactive calls to @code{vm-save-message}
to ask for confirmation before creating a new folder.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmconvertfoldertypes
@defopt vm-convert-folder-types
@vindex vm-convert-folder-types
Non-nil value means that when VM checks folder types and finds
a mismatch (see @code{vm-check-folder-types}), it will convert the
source messages to the type of the destination folder, if it can.

If @code{vm-check-folder-types} is nil, then this variable isn’t
consulted.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcrashbox
@defopt vm-crash-box
@vindex vm-crash-box
File in which to store mail temporarily while it is transferred from
the system mailbox to the primary inbox.  If a crash occurs
during this mail transfer, any missing mail will be found in this
file.  VM will do crash recovery from this file automatically at
startup, as necessary.

If the variable is to nil, a crash box name is created by appending
@code{vm-primary-inbox} and @code{vm-crash-box-suffix}.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcrashboxsuffix
@defopt vm-crash-box-suffix
@vindex vm-crash-box-suffix
String suffix used to create possible crash box file names for folders.
When VM uses @code{vm-spool-file-suffixes} to create a spool file name,
it will append the value of @code{vm-crash-box-suffix} to the folder’s
file name to create a crash box name.

Default value: @code{".crash"}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmdefaultFromfoldertype
@defopt vm-default-From_-folder-type
@vindex vm-default-From_-folder-type
Value must be a symbol that tells VM which From-style folder type
is used by your local mail delivery system.  Valid values are

@example
    From_
    BellFrom_

@end example
Messages in From_ folders are separated by the two newlines
followed by the string "From" and a space.  Messages in
BellFrom_ folders are only required to have a single newline
before the "From" string.

Since BellFrom_ and From_ folders cannot be reliably distinguished
from each other, you must tell VM which one your system uses by
setting the variable @code{vm-default-From_-folder-type} to either From_
or BellFrom_.

Default value: @code{From_}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmdefaultfolderpermissionbits
@defopt vm-default-folder-permission-bits
@vindex vm-default-folder-permission-bits
Default UNIX permission bits for newly created folders.

Default value: @code{384}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmdefaultfoldertype
@defopt vm-default-folder-type
@vindex vm-default-folder-type
Default folder type for empty folders.
If VM has to add messages that have no specific folder type to an
empty folder, the folder will become this default type.

It decides a folder VM creates, and nothing else: an existing folder is read
as what its name says, per @code{vm-folder-type-by-extension-alist}, or, where the
name says nothing, as what VM can work out from its contents.  So changing
this cannot change how a folder you already have is read.

Set to mboxcl2 it decides the name as well: a folder VM creates is created as
NAME.mboxcl2.  A folder’s type is read back from its name, so mboxcl2 written
under a name that says nothing would be read as From_ next time and split
wherever a body line begins "From ".  See @code{vm-new-folder-file-name}.

It was mboxcl2 on Solaris, AIX and System V and mmdf on SCO until 2026: a
guess about what the local delivery agent writes, made when VM could not be
told.  Say what you want in the name of the folder, or here.

Allowed types are:

@example
   From_
   mboxcl2
   mmdf
   babyl

@end example
@code{mboxcl2} was called @code{From_-with-Content-Length} until 2026 and is still
accepted under that name.

@code{BellFrom_} is not among them, and has not been offered since 2026.  VM reads
one it is handed, and creates none: that format is From_ without the blank
line between messages, so it has no signature of its own, and a folder VM
wrote as one is read back as From_ with its messages run together.  Setting
it here still works and still has that result, so VM says so as it starts.
Issue #787.  It is the one mbox variant that stores a message
exactly as it arrived, and so the one to choose for a folder kept as a
record.

Value must be a symbol, not a string. i.e. write

  (setq vm-default-folder-type 'From_)

in your .emacs or .vm file.

If you set this variable’s value to mboxcl2 you
must set @code{vm-trust-content-length} non-nil.

Default value: @code{From_}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmdefaultnewfolderlineendingtype
@defopt vm-default-new-folder-line-ending-type
@vindex vm-default-new-folder-line-ending-type
Value must be a symbol that specifies the line ending convention
to use for new folders.  Text files under UNIXish and Windows
systems use different characters to indicate the end of a line.
UNIXish systems use a single linefeed character, Windows uses a
carriage return followed by a line feed.  The value of this
variable tells VM which to use.

@code{nil} means use the line ending convention of the local system;
CRLF if you’re on a Windows system, LF for UNIXish systems.
@code{crlf} means use CRLF.
@code{lf} mean use LF.
@code{cr} means use CR (old Macs use this).
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmdeleteemptyfolders
@defopt vm-delete-empty-folders
@vindex vm-delete-empty-folders
Non-nil value means remove empty (zero length) folders after saving.
A value of t means always remove the folders.
A value of nil means never remove empty folders.
A value that’s not t or nil means ask before removing empty folders.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmflushinterval
@defopt vm-flush-interval
@vindex vm-flush-interval
Non-nil value specifies how often VM flushes its cached
internal data using a timer task.  A numeric value gives the
number of seconds between flushes.  A value of t means flush
every time there is a change.  Nil means don’t do flushing until
a message or folder is saved.

Normally when a message attribute is changed. VM keeps the record
of the change in its internal memory and doesn’t insert the
changed data into the folder buffer until a particular message or
the whole folder is saved to disk.  This makes normal Emacs
auto-saving useless for VM folder buffers because the information
you’d want to auto-save, i.e. the attribute changes are not in
the buffer when it is auto-saved.

Setting @code{vm-flush-interval} to a numeric value will cause the VM’s
internal memory caches to be periodically flushed to the folder
buffer.  This is done non-obtrusively, so that if you type
something while flushing is occurring, the flush will abort
cleanly and Emacs will respond to your keystrokes as usual.

Default value: @code{90}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmfolderdirectory
@defopt vm-folder-directory
@vindex vm-folder-directory
Directory where folders of mail are kept.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmfolderfilepreciousflag
@defopt vm-folder-file-precious-flag
@vindex vm-folder-file-precious-flag
Value that @code{file-precious-flag} should have in visited folders.
A non-nil value causes folders to be saved by writing to a
temporary file and then replacing the folder with that file.  A
nil value causes folders to be saved by writing directly to the
folder without the use of a temporary file.

The default protects a folder against a crash part way through a save,
which would otherwise leave it truncated.  It has one cost: replacing the
folder replaces its name, so a folder that is a *hard link* is left with
the other names pointing at the old contents -- see issue #532.  Nothing
can preserve a hard link and replace a file atomically at the same time,
so set this to nil for folders kept as hard links, accepting the weaker
guarantee against an interrupted save.

Symbolic links are not affected; VM keeps those either way.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmfoldertypebyextensionalist
@defopt vm-folder-type-by-extension-alist
@vindex vm-folder-type-by-extension-alist
Alist of (EXTENSION . TYPE): the folder type a folder’s name asks for.
EXTENSION is matched literally against the file name’s extension, without
the dot, and TYPE is one of the types @code{vm-default-folder-type} accepts.

This is consulted where a folder cannot say for itself what it is:

@example
  - a folder that does not exist yet, or is empty, is created in the type
    its name asks for rather than in ‘vm-default-folder-type’;
  - a From_ folder is read as the type its name gives.  From_ and mboxcl2
    are the same folder but for the ‘Content-Length’ header, so looking
    like one is no evidence: a folder named mboxcl2 is mboxcl2, and a
    message in it that has no length is then something the reader
    complains about -- which is the use of saying so in the name.

@end example
What a folder’s own contents say is never overridden where they settle the
question: a name ending .mboxcl2 does not make VM read a BABYL file as one.

.mbox is From_, which is what the rest of the world means by an mbox file.
From_ is also the type a folder has when its name says nothing about it, so it
is the one type an extension names without being the name that type is written
under: a folder called sent.mbox keeps that name when converted to From_, and
one called INBOX is not renamed INBOX.mbox by the same conversion.  See
@code{vm-folder-type-with-no-name-of-its-own}.

Not .mboxcl: VM has no mboxcl type, and mboxcl quotes "From " lines in bodies
where mboxcl2 does not, so reading one as the other would misread exactly
those bodies.

An extension and not a pattern over the name.  A pattern can be written so
that it matches nothing, silently, and it can be written so that it claims
every folder in a directory -- and a directory of nine From_ folders claimed
as mboxcl2 is nine folders VM then refuses to read.  The cost is that a
folder which cannot be renamed cannot be typed by its name; that is what
@code{vm-default-folder-type} is for.

Set it to nil to have names mean nothing, which is what VM did before.

Default value: @code{(("mboxcl2" . mboxcl2) ("mbox" . From_))}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmimapautoexpungealist
@defopt vm-imap-auto-expunge-alist
@vindex vm-imap-auto-expunge-alist
List of IMAP mailboxes and values specifying whether messages
should be automatically deleted from the mailbox after retrieval.
The format of the list is

  ((MAILBOX . VAL) (MAILBOX . VAL) ...)

MAILBOX should be an IMAP mailbox specification as described in
the documentation for the variable @code{vm-spool-files}.  If you have
the IMAP password specified in the @code{vm-spool-files} entry, you do
not have to specify it here as well.  Use @code{*} instead; VM will
still understand that this mailbox is the same as the one in
@code{vm-spool-files} that contains the password.

VAL should be nil if retrieved messages should be left in the
corresponding IMAP mailbox, t if retrieved messages should be
deleted from the mailbox immediately after retrieval.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmimapbytespersession
@defopt vm-imap-bytes-per-session
@vindex vm-imap-bytes-per-session
Non-nil value should be an integer specifying how many bytes to
retrieve per IMAP session.  When you type @code{g} to get new mail, VM
will only retrieve messages until the byte limit is reached on
any particular IMAP maildrop.  To retrieve more messages, type @code{g}
again.

A nil value means there’s no limit.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmimapexpungeafterretrieving
@defopt vm-imap-expunge-after-retrieving
@vindex vm-imap-expunge-after-retrieving
Non-nil value means that, when an IMAP mailbox is used as a
spool file, messages should be deleted after retrieving them.  A
nil value means messages will be left in the IMAP mailbox until
you run @code{vm-expunge-imap-messages}.

This variable only affects IMAP mailboxes not listed in
@code{vm-imap-auto-expunge-alist} (which is the recommended method for
customizing this behavior).
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmimapmessagespersession
@defopt vm-imap-messages-per-session
@vindex vm-imap-messages-per-session
Non-nil value should be an integer specifying how many messages to
retrieve per IMAP session.  When you type @code{g} to get new mail, VM
will only retrieve that many messages from any particular IMAP maildrop.
To retrieve more messages, type @code{g} again.

A nil value means there’s no limit.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmindexfilesuffix
@defopt vm-index-file-suffix
@vindex vm-index-file-suffix
Suffix used to construct VM index file names, e.g., ".inx".
When VM visits a folder, it checks for the existence of a file
whose name is the folder’s file name with the value of this
variable appended to it.  If found, the file’s contents will be
used to tell VM about the contents of the folder.  This is faster
than parsing the folder itself.

When you save a folder, the index file will be rewritten with
updated information about the folder.

A nil value means VM should not read or write index files.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmkeepcrashboxes
@defopt vm-keep-crash-boxes
@vindex vm-keep-crash-boxes
Non-nil value should be a string specifying a directory where
your crash boxes should be moved after VM has copied new mail
out of them.  This is a safety measure.  In at least one case a
pointer corruption bug inside Emacs has caused VM to believe that
it had copied information out of the crash box when it in fact
had not.  VM then deleted the crash box, losing the batch of
incoming mail.  This is an exceedingly rare problem, but if you
want to avoid losing mail if it happens, set @code{vm-keep-crash-boxes}
to point to a directory in the same filesystem as all your
crash boxes.  Each saved crash box will have a unique name based
on the current date and time the box was saved.  You will need to
clean out this directory from time to time; VM does not do so.

A nil value means VM should just delete crash boxes after it
has copied out the mail.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailcheckalways
@defopt vm-mail-check-always
@vindex vm-mail-check-always
Set this variable to @code{t} if you want VM’s mail-check to run
continuously and take into account multiple mail clients reading from
the same mail spool.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailcheckinterval
@defopt vm-mail-check-interval
@vindex vm-mail-check-interval
Numeric value specifies the number of seconds between checks
for new mail, carried out using a timer task.  The maildrops for all
visited folders are checked. 

A nil value means don’t check for new mail.

Note that if new mail is found, it is not retrieved.  The
buffer local variable @code{vm-spooled-mail-waiting} is set non-nil in
the buffers of those folders that have mail waiting.  VM
displays "Mail" in the mode line of folders that have mail
waiting.

Default value: @code{300}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmakecrashboxname
@defopt vm-make-crash-box-name
@vindex vm-make-crash-box-name
Non-nil value should be a function that returns a crash box file name
for a folder.  The function will be called with one argument, the
folder’s file name.  If the folder does not have a file name,
the function will not be called.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmakespoolfilename
@defopt vm-make-spool-file-name
@vindex vm-make-spool-file-name
Non-nil value should be a function that returns a spool file name
for a folder.  The function will be called with one argument, the
folder’s file name.  If the folder does not have a file name,
the function will not be called.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmboxcl2strict
@defopt vm-mboxcl2-strict
@vindex vm-mboxcl2-strict
Non-nil means refuse to read an mboxcl2 message that has no length.
An mboxcl2 folder stores each message’s length in a @code{Content-Length} header
and that is how the next message is found.  A message written into such a
folder without one -- by another mailer, or by VM before this was checked --
leaves the folder saying one thing and containing another.

With this set, reading such a folder signals an error naming the message and
saying how to repair it: @code{vm-change-folder-type} with a prefix argument
converts a folder on disk, without visiting it, and gives every message a
length.

With it nil, VM warns and falls back on looking for the next line beginning
"From ", which is what it used to do silently.  Turning it off to get at a
broken folder is not necessary and is easy to forget about; the repair does
not need it.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmovemessagesphysically
@defopt vm-move-messages-physically
@vindex vm-move-messages-physically
Non-nil value causes VM’s commands that change the message order
of a folder to always move the physical messages involved and not
just change the presentation order.  Nil means that commands just
change the order in which VM displays messages and leave the
folder itself undisturbed.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopautoexpungealist
@defopt vm-pop-auto-expunge-alist
@vindex vm-pop-auto-expunge-alist
List of POP mailboxes and values specifying whether messages
should be automatically deleted from the mailbox after retrieval.
The format of the list is

  ((MAILBOX . VAL) (MAILBOX . VAL) ...)

MAILBOX should be a POP mailbox specification as described in
the documentation for the variable @code{vm-spool-files}.  If you have
the POP password specified in the @code{vm-spool-files} entry, you do
not have to specify it here as well.  Use @code{*} instead; VM will
still understand that this mailbox is the same as the one in
@code{vm-spool-files} that gives the password.

VAL should be nil if retrieved messages should be left in the
corresponding POP mailbox, t if retrieved messages should be
deleted from the mailbox immediately after retrieval.

VM can only support a non-nil setting of this variable if the
remote POP server supports the UIDL command.  If the server does
not support UIDL and you’ve asked to VM leave messages on the server,
VM will complain about the lack of UIDL support and not retrieve
messages from the server.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopbytespersession
@defopt vm-pop-bytes-per-session
@vindex vm-pop-bytes-per-session
Non-nil value should be an integer specifying how many bytes to
retrieve per POP session.  When you type @code{g} to get new mail, VM
will only retrieve messages until the byte limit is reached on
any particular POP maildrop.  To retrieve more messages, type @code{g}
again.

A nil value means there’s no limit.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopexpungeafterretrieving
@defopt vm-pop-expunge-after-retrieving
@vindex vm-pop-expunge-after-retrieving
Non-nil value means that, when a POP mailbox is used as a
spool file, messages should be deleted after retrieving them.  A
nil value means messages will be left in the POP mailbox until
you run @code{vm-expunge-pop-messages}.  VM can only support a nil
value for this variable if the remote POP server supports the
UIDL command.  If the server does not support UIDL and you’ve
asked VM leave messages on the server, VM will complain about the
lack of UIDL support and not retrieve messages from the server.

This variable only affects POP mailboxes not listed in
@code{vm-pop-auto-expunge-alist} (which is the recommended method for
customizing this behavior).
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopmessagespersession
@defopt vm-pop-messages-per-session
@vindex vm-pop-messages-per-session
Non-nil value should be an integer specifying how many messages to
retrieve per POP session.  When you type @code{g} to get new mail, VM
will only retrieve that many messages from any particular POP maildrop.
To retrieve more messages, type @code{g} again.

A nil value means there’s no limit.
@end defopt

@end macro

