@macro vmdocvmicalendarshowdescription
@defopt vm-icalendar-show-description
@vindex vm-icalendar-show-description
Whether a calendar part’s DESCRIPTION is shown along with the rest.
The description of an invitation is often several screens of dial-in
numbers and legal boilerplate, and the parts worth reading -- what, when,
where, who -- come before it.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvminfermimetypes
@defopt vm-infer-mime-types
@vindex vm-infer-mime-types
Non-nil value means that VM should try to infer a MIME object’s
type from its filename when deciding whether the object should be
displayed and how it should be displayed.  This will be done only
for objects of type application/octet-stream.  The object’s filename
is checked against the regexps in @code{vm-mime-attachment-auto-type-alist}
and the type corresponding to the first match found is used.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvminfermimetypesfortext
@defopt vm-infer-mime-types-for-text
@vindex vm-infer-mime-types-for-text
Non-nil value means VM should try to infer a MIME object’s
@example
  type from its filename also for text attachments, not only for
  application/octet-stream.
@end example
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmime8bitheadercharsets
@defopt vm-mime-8bit-header-charsets
@vindex vm-mime-8bit-header-charsets
Coding systems to try on header text that arrives as raw 8-bit bytes.
RFC 5322 allows only ASCII in a header and RFC 2047 provides encoded words
for everything else, but plenty of mail carries 8-bit header text anyway,
and RFC 6532 makes UTF-8 legal there.  Such text says nothing about its own
character set, so VM tries these coding systems in order and takes the first
that decodes the whole of the text.

The order matters: UTF-8 first, because a byte sequence that is valid UTF-8
is almost never anything else, and a single-byte encoding after it, since
that cannot fail and so nothing is left undecoded.  Set this to nil to leave
raw 8-bit header text exactly as it arrives, which is what VM did before.

Default value: @code{(utf-8 iso-8859-1)}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmime8bittexttransferencoding
@defopt vm-mime-8bit-text-transfer-encoding
@vindex vm-mime-8bit-text-transfer-encoding
Symbol specifying what kind of transfer encoding to use on 8bit
text.  Characters with the high bit set cannot safely pass
through all mail gateways and mail transport software.  MIME has
two transfer encodings that convert 8-bit data to 7-bit for safe
transport. Quoted-printable leaves the text mostly readable even
if the recipient does not have a MIME-capable mail reader.  BASE64
is unreadable without a MIME-capable mail reader, unless your name
is U3BvY2s=.

A value of @code{quoted-printable}, means to use quoted-printable encoding.
A value of @code{base64} means to use BASE64 encoding.
A value of @code{8bit} means to send the message as is.

Note that this variable usually only applies to textual MIME
content types.  Images, audio, video, etc. typically will have
some attribute that makes VM consider them to be "binary",
which moves them outside the scope of this variable.  For
example, messages with line lengths of 1000 characters or more
are considered binary, as are messages that contain carriage
returns (ascii code 13) or NULs (ascii code 0).

Default value: @code{quoted-printable}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmimeallattachmentsdirectory
@defopt vm-mime-all-attachments-directory
@vindex vm-mime-all-attachments-directory
Directory to where the attachments should go or come from.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmimealternativeshowmethod
@defopt vm-mime-alternative-show-method
@vindex vm-mime-alternative-show-method
Value tells how to choose which alternative to display when
it displays a message with "multipart/alternative" content.
Possible values are @code{best}, @code{best-internal}, @code{all}, or a
favorite-methods list as described below.

  A MIME message of type multipart/alternative has multiple
message parts containing the same information, but each part may
be formatted differently.  VM will typically display only one of
the parts.  This variable tells VM how to choose which part to
display.

  (There is a separate variable @code{vm-mime-alternative-yank-method}
for deciding the multipart/alternative to be used in replies.)

  A value of @code{best} means choose the part that is the most
faithful to the sender’s original content that can be displayed.

  A value of @code{best-internal} means choose the best part that can
be displayed internally, (i.e. with the built-in capabilities of
Emacs) and is allowed to be displayed internally (see
@code{vm-mime-internal-content-types}).  If none of the parts can be
displayed internally, behavior reverts to that of @code{best}.

  A value of @code{all} means that all the alternatives are displayed.

  The value can also be a list of the form

  (favorite TYPE ...)

with the first element of the list being the symbol @code{favorite}.  The
remaining elements of the list are strings specifying MIME types.
VM will look for each TYPE in turn in the list of alternatives and
choose the first matching alternative found that can be displayed.

If the symbol @code{favorite} is @code{favorite-internal} instead, the first TYPE
that matches an alternative that can be displayed internally will be
chosen.

Default value: @code{best-internal}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmimealternativeyankmethod
@defopt vm-mime-alternative-yank-method
@vindex vm-mime-alternative-yank-method
Value tells how to choose which alternative to yank, i.e.,
include, in replies, when it yanks a message with
"multipart/alternative" content.  (It is similar to
@code{vm-mime-alternative-show-method} used for displaying messages.)
Possible values are @code{best}, @code{best-internal}, @code{all}, or a
favorite-methods list as described below.

  A value of @code{best} means choose the part that is the most faithful to
the sender’s original content that can be displayed.

  A value of @code{best-internal} means choose the best part that can
be displayed internally, (i.e. with the built-in capabilities of
Emacs) and is allowed to be displayed internally (see
@code{vm-mime-internal-content-types}).  If none of the parts can be
displayed internally, behavior reverts to that of @code{best}.

  A value of @code{all} means that all the alternatives are yanked.

  The value can also be a list of the form

  (favorite TYPE ...)

with the first element of the list being the symbol @code{favorite}.  The
remaining elements of the list are strings specifying MIME types.
VM will look for each TYPE in turn in the list of alternatives and
choose the first matching alternative found that can be displayed.

If the symbol @code{favorite} is @code{favorite-internal} instead, the first TYPE
that matches an alternative that can be displayed internally will be
chosen.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmimeattachmentautosuffixalist
@defopt vm-mime-attachment-auto-suffix-alist
@vindex vm-mime-attachment-auto-suffix-alist
Alist used to select a filename suffix for MIME object temporary files.
The list format is

  ((TYPE . SUFFIX) ...)

TYPE is a string specifying a MIME top-level type or a type/subtype pair.
If a top-level type is listed without a subtype, all subtypes of
that type are matched.

SUFFIX is a string specifying the suffix that should be used for
the accompanying type.

When a MIME object is displayed using an external viewer VM must
first write the object to a temporary file.  The external viewer
opens and displays that file.  Some viewers will not open a file
unless the filename ends with some extension that it recognizes
such as ’.html’ or ’.jpg’.  You can use this variable to map MIME
types to extensions that your external viewers will recognize.  VM
will search the list for a matching type.  The suffix associated
with the first type that matches will be used.

Default value:
@example
(("image/jpeg" . ".jpg") ("image/gif" . ".gif") ("image/png" . ".png")
 ("image/tiff" . ".tif") ("text/html" . ".html")
 ("audio/basic" . ".au") ("video/mpeg" . ".mpg")
 ("video/quicktime" . ".mov") ("application/zip" . ".zip")
 ("application/postscript" . ".ps") ("application/pdf" . ".pdf")
 ("application/msword" . ".doc") ("application/vnd.ms-excel" . ".xls")
 ("application/vnd.ms-powerpoint" . ".ppt")
 ("application/mac-binhex40" . ".hqx"))
@end example
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmimeattachmentautotypealist
@defopt vm-mime-attachment-auto-type-alist
@vindex vm-mime-attachment-auto-type-alist
Alist used to guess a MIME content type based on a file name.
The list format is

  ((REGEXP . TYPE) ...)

REGEXP is a string that specifies a regular expression.
TYPE is a string specifying a MIME content type.

When a file is attached to a MIME composition buffer using
@code{vm-attach-file}, this list will be scanned until a REGEXP
matches the file’s name.  The corresponding TYPE will be
offered as a default when you are prompted for the file’s
type.

The value of this variable is also used to guess the MIME type of
application/octet-stream objects for display purposes if the
value of @code{vm-infer-mime-types} is non-nil.

A suffix that is not listed here is looked up in Emacs’s mailcap tables,
which read the system’s /etc/mime.types: .org is text/x-org there and
