@macro vmdocvmsmimeencryptmessage
@deffn Command vm-smime-encrypt-message
@findex vm-smime-encrypt-message
Toggle the current composition for S/MIME encryption. This
only sets a flag and will not do the encryption immediately.
Actual encryption is done upon sending the message. If the
message is already set for encryption this function will clear
the flag so that no encryption is done
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsmimesignmessage
@deffn Command vm-smime-sign-message
@findex vm-smime-sign-message
Toggle the current composition for S/MIME signing. This only
sets a flag and will not do the signing immediately.  The signing
itself is done upon sending the message. If the message is
already set for signing this function will clear the flag so
that no signing is done
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmv8keybindings
@deffn Command vm-v8-key-bindings
@findex vm-v8-key-bindings
Install the key bindings VM 8 gives its own commands.

Nothing needs to call this: the bindings are in @code{vm-mode-map} and
@code{vm-mode-virtual-map} to begin with.  It is here because the manual told
readers to put it in their preferences file, back when these keys were not
bound and typing one reported that it had an optional binding
(emacs-vm/vm#632).  Calling it binds what is bound already.

@emph{Not autoloaded}: VM has to be loaded before @kbd{M-x} offers this one.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmviewfileotherframe
@deffn Command vm-view-file-other-frame 91 65 114 103 32 108 105 115 116 32 110 111 116 32 97 118 97 105 108 97 98 108 101 32 117 110 116 105 108 32 102 117 110 99 116 105 111 110 32 100 101 102 105 110 105 116 105 111 110 32 105 115 32 108 111 97 100 101 100 46 93
@findex vm-view-file-other-frame
View FILE in View mode in another frame.
When done, kill the buffer visiting FILE if unmodified and if it wasn’t
visited before; also, maybe delete other frame and/or return to previous
buffer.

Emacs commands editing the buffer contents are not available; instead,
a special set of commands (mostly letters and punctuation)
are defined for moving around in the buffer.
Space scrolls forward, Delete scrolls backward.
For a list of all View commands, type H or h while viewing.

This command runs the normal hook @code{view-mode-hook}.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmdofccbeforemimeencode
@deffn Command vm-do-fcc-before-mime-encode
@findex vm-do-fcc-before-mime-encode
The name says it all.
Sometimes you may want to save a message unencoded, specifically not to waste
storage for attachments which are stored on disk anyway.

@emph{Not autoloaded}: VM has to be loaded before @kbd{M-x} offers this one.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmautodisplayedmimecontenttypeexceptions
@defopt vm-auto-displayed-mime-content-type-exceptions
@vindex vm-auto-displayed-mime-content-type-exceptions
List of MIME content types that should not be displayed immediately
after decoding.  These types will be displayed as a button that you
must activate to display the object.  This is an exception list for
the types listed in @code{vm-mime-auto-displayed-content-types}; all types
listed there will be auto-displayed except those in the exception
list.

The value should be either nil or a list of strings.  The strings
should all be types or type/subtype pairs.  Example:

 (setq vm-mime-auto-displayed-content-type-exceptions '("text/html"))

If a top-level type is listed without a subtype, all subtypes of
that type are assumed to be included.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmautodisplayedmimecontenttypes
@defopt vm-auto-displayed-mime-content-types
@vindex vm-auto-displayed-mime-content-types
List of MIME content types that should be displayed immediately
after decoding.  Other types will be displayed as a button that
you must activate to display the object.

A value of t means that all types should be displayed immediately.
A nil value means never display MIME objects immediately; only use buttons.

If the value is a list, it should be a list of strings, which
should all be types or type/subtype pairs.  Example:

 (setq vm-mime-auto-displayed-content-types '("text" "image/jpeg"))

If a top-level type is listed without a subtype, all subtypes of
that type are assumed to be included.

Note that all multipart types are processed specially, and this
variable does not apply to them.  In particular,

@example
   multipart/digest messages are always displayed as a button to
   avoid automatically visiting a new folder while you are moving
   around in the current folder.

   message/partial messages are always displayed as a button,
   because there always needs to be a way to trigger the assembly
   of the parts into a full message.

@end example
Any type that cannot be displayed internally or externally will
be displayed as a button that allows you to save the body of the MIME
object to a file.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmconfirmquit
@defopt vm-confirm-quit
@vindex vm-confirm-quit
Value of t causes VM to always ask for confirmation before quitting
a VM visit of a folder.  A nil value means VM will ask only when messages
will be lost unwittingly by quitting, i.e. not removed by intentional
delete and expunge.  A value that is not nil and not t causes VM to ask
only when there are unsaved changes to message attributes, or when messages
will be unwittingly lost.

Default value: @code{if-something-will-be-lost}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmexpungebeforequit
@defopt vm-expunge-before-quit
@vindex vm-expunge-before-quit
Non-nil value causes VM to expunge deleted messages before
quitting.  You can use @code{vm-quit-no-expunge} and @code{vm-quit-no-change}
to override this behavior.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmfetchedmessagelimit
@defopt vm-fetched-message-limit
@vindex vm-fetched-message-limit
Should be an integer representing the maximum number of messages
that VM should keep in the Folder buffer when the messages are
fetched on demand, or nil to signify no limit.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhandlereturnreceiptmode
@defopt vm-handle-return-receipt-mode
@vindex vm-handle-return-receipt-mode
Tells @code{vm-handle-return-receipt} how to handle return receipts.
One can choose between @code{ask}, @code{auto}, @code{edit}, or an expression which should
return t if the return receipts should be sent.

Default value: @code{edit}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhandlereturnreceiptpeek
@defopt vm-handle-return-receipt-peek
@vindex vm-handle-return-receipt-peek
Number of characters from the original message body to be returned.

Default value: @code{500}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhandlereturnreceipts
@defopt vm-handle-return-receipts
@vindex vm-handle-return-receipts
Non-nil means answer a message asking for a return receipt.
@code{vm-handle-return-receipt-mode} says whether the answer is sent as it
stands, asked about first, or left in a composition buffer for you.

A return receipt is a request, not an instruction: a sender cannot make
your mail reader tell them you read something, and VM does not by default.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhonormimecontentdisposition
@defopt vm-honor-mime-content-disposition
@vindex vm-honor-mime-content-disposition
Non-nil value means use information from the Content-Disposition
header to display MIME messages.  Possible values are @code{t}, to mean that the
Content-Disposition header should always be honored or @code{internal-only},
to mean that an "inline" disposition should be honored only for
internally-displayable types. 

The Content-Disposition header specifies whether a MIME object
should be displayed inline or treated as an attachment.  For VM,
"inline" display means displaying the object in the Emacs
buffer, if possible.  Attachments will be displayed as a button
that you can use mouse-2 to activate or mouse-3 to pull up a menu
of options.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmimagedirectory
@defopt vm-image-directory
@vindex vm-image-directory
The directory where VM finds the pixmaps for mime objects.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvminitfile
@defopt vm-init-file
@vindex vm-init-file
Startup file for VM that is loaded the first time you run VM
in an Emacs session.

Default value: @code{"~/.vm"}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmloglevel
@defopt vm-log-level
@vindex vm-log-level
Level up to which VM records what it is doing, beyond what it shows.
Everything VM says is recorded in the buffer *VM Log* whatever this is set
to; a nil value means nothing more than that.  @code{M-x vm-show-log} shows the
buffer.

The scale is @code{vm-verbosity}’s.  Recording at 10 and leaving @code{vm-verbosity}
alone is how to keep the detail of a slow operation without the echo area
churn, and without @code{vm-verbal-time} pausing for each message.

Each line says when VM said it, how long it is since the previous line and
how much CPU VM used in between:

  14:03:12.481 +2.140s +0.310cpu [6] INBOX: Retrieving message 400...

An interval that is all real time and no CPU was spent waiting for a server,
which is what says whether something slow is VM’s own doing.  The interval
is simply the time since the last recorded message, so it counts time VM sat
idle as readily as time it worked.

@code{vm-log-max-lines} bounds the buffer.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmlogmaxlines
@defopt vm-log-max-lines
@vindex vm-log-max-lines
How many lines of *VM Log* to keep, or nil to keep all of them.
The log runs for as long as Emacs does, so something has to bound it, and
what a reader wants is the end: the oldest lines go first.

Default value: @code{20000}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmimesavabletypeexceptions
@defopt vm-mime-savable-type-exceptions
@vindex vm-mime-savable-type-exceptions
List of MIME types which should not be saved.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmimesavabletypes
@defopt vm-mime-savable-types
@vindex vm-mime-savable-types
List of MIME types which should be saved.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmutableframes
@defopt vm-mutable-frames
@vindex vm-mutable-frames
Non-nil value means VM is allowed to create and destroy frames
to display and undisplay buffers.  Whether VM actually does
so depends on the value of the variables with names prefixed by
@code{‘vm-frame-per-}’.

VM can create a frame to display a buffer, and delete frame to
undisplay a buffer.  A nil value means VM should not create or
delete frames.

This variable does not apply to the VM commands whose
names end in -other-frame, which always create a new frame.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmutablewindows
@defopt vm-mutable-windows
@vindex vm-mutable-windows
This variable’s value controls VM’s window usage.

A non-nil value gives VM free run of the Emacs display; it will commandeer
the entire screen for its purposes.

A value of nil restricts VM’s window usage to the window from which
it was invoked.  VM will not create, delete, or use any other windows,
nor will it resize its own window.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpreferencesfile
@defopt vm-preferences-file
@vindex vm-preferences-file
Secondary startup file for VM, loaded after @code{vm-init-file}.  It is
meant for specifying the preferred settings for VM variables.

Default value: @code{"~/.vm.preferences"}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpreservereadonlyfoldersondisk
@defopt vm-preserve-read-only-folders-on-disk
@vindex vm-preserve-read-only-folders-on-disk
Non-nil value means that VM will not attempt to save read-only
folders to disk and discards changes during @code{vm-quit}.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmreportsubprocesserrors
@defopt vm-report-subprocess-errors
@vindex vm-report-subprocess-errors
Boolean flag that controls whether VM should report errors from
running commands in subprocesses.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsearchusingregexps
@defopt vm-search-using-regexps
@vindex vm-search-using-regexps
Non-nil value causes VM’s search command to interpret user input as a
regular expression instead of as a literal string.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsuggestcheckingconfiguration
@defopt vm-suggest-checking-configuration
@vindex vm-suggest-checking-configuration
Non-nil means say once per session that the configuration is incomplete.
One line in the echo area at the first VM start, naming
vm-check-configuration, where that command would have something to
report.  It never runs the check itself and never puts up a buffer: someone
who has deliberately left a setting alone should not be shown a report they
did not ask for.

Nil says nothing.  Nothing is said either way once the settings VM checks
are in place, so this is quiet on a working configuration.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsummaryuninterestingsendersarrow
@defopt vm-summary-uninteresting-senders-arrow
@vindex vm-summary-uninteresting-senders-arrow
String to display before the recipients when displayed instead of an
"uninteresting" sender.  See @code{vm-summary-uninteresting-senders}.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmtempfiledirectory
@defopt vm-temp-file-directory
@vindex vm-temp-file-directory
Name of a directory where VM can put temporary files.

Default value: worked out when VM is loaded, from this system.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmverbaltime
@defopt vm-verbal-time
@vindex vm-verbal-time
Number of seconds for which to display VM’s minibuffer messages.
This number should be normally 0.  Otherwise, it will delay VM’s
operation.

Default value: @code{0}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmverbosity
@defopt vm-verbosity
@vindex vm-verbosity
Level of chattiness in progress messages displayed in the
minibuffer.  A message is shown when its own level is this or lower, so a
larger number here means more talk.  The scale runs from 0 to 10:

@example
  0 - only what VM cannot stay silent about
  1 - errors, and warnings worth interrupting for
  5 - normal level: what a command did, and the progress of anything slow
      enough to be worth watching
  6 - the steps within those operations
  7 - detail: threading, summary generation, parsing, header work
  8 - more of the same, and VM’s own bookkeeping
@end example
 10 - debugging information

@code{vm-warn} uses the same scale, so lowering this hides warnings as well as
progress.  Nothing above 1 is a warning.

Default value: @code{5}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmvsspamscoreheaders
@defopt vm-vs-spam-score-headers
@vindex vm-vs-spam-score-headers
The value should be a list of lists, with each sublist of the form

     (HEADER-REGEXP SCORE-REGEXP SCORE-FN)

- HEADER-REGEXP is a regular expression matching the spam score
header line in email messages,

- SCORE-REGEXP is a regular expression matching the score, and

- SCORE-FN is a function that converts the score string into a number.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmexpungepopmessages
@deffn Command vm-expunge-pop-messages
@findex vm-expunge-pop-messages
Deletes all messages from POP mailbox that have already been retrieved
into the current folder.  VM sends POP DELE commands to all the
relevant POP servers to remove the messages.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopstartbugreport
@deffn Command vm-pop-start-bug-report
@findex vm-pop-start-bug-report
Begin to compose a bug report for POP support functionality.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopsubmitbugreport
@deffn Command vm-pop-submit-bug-report
@findex vm-pop-submit-bug-report
Submit a bug report for VM’s POP support functionality.  
It is necessary to run @code{vm-pop-start-bug-report} before the problem
occurrence and this command after the problem occurrence, in
order to capture the trace of POP sessions during the occurrence.

The session still running is included, so a report can be made about a fetch
while it is happening; nothing is closed to collect it.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopfolderalist
@defopt vm-pop-folder-alist
@vindex vm-pop-folder-alist
Alist of POP maildrop specifications and names that refer to them.
The alist format is:

 ((POPDROP NAME) ...)

POPDROP is a POP maildrop specification in the same format used
by @code{vm-spool-files} (which see).

NAME is a string that should give a less cumbersome name that you
will use to refer to this maildrop when using @code{vm-visit-pop-folder}.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopfoldercachedirectory
@defopt vm-pop-folder-cache-directory
@vindex vm-pop-folder-cache-directory
Directory where VM stores cached copies of POP folders.
When VM visits a POP folder (really just a POP server where you
have a mailbox) it stores the retrieved message on your computer
so that they need not be retrieved each time you visit the folder.
The cached copies are stored in the directory specified by this
variable.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopkeeptracebuffer
@defopt vm-pop-keep-trace-buffer
@vindex vm-pop-keep-trace-buffer
The number of POP session trace buffers that should be
@example
  retained for debugging purposes.  If it is nil, then no trace
  buffers are kept.
@end example

Default value: @code{1}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopmaxmessagesize
@defopt vm-pop-max-message-size
@vindex vm-pop-max-message-size
If VM is about to retrieve via POP a message larger than this
size (in bytes) it will ask the you whether it should retrieve
the message.

If VM is retrieving mail automatically because @code{vm-auto-get-new-mail}
is set to a numeric value then you will not be prompted about large
messages.  This is to avoid prompting you while you’re typing in
another buffer.  In this case the large message will be skipped with a
warning message.  You will be able to retrieved any skipped messages
later by running @code{vm-get-new-mail} interactively.

A nil value for @code{vm-pop-max-message-size} means no size limit.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopreadquitresponse
@defopt vm-pop-read-quit-response
@vindex vm-pop-read-quit-response
Non-nil value tells VM to read the response to the POP QUIT command.
Sometimes, for reasons unknown, the QUIT response never arrives
from some POP servers and VM will hang waiting for it.  So it is
useful to be able to tell VM not to wait.  Some other
servers will not expunge messages unless the QUIT response is
read, so for these servers you should set the variable’s value to
t.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpopservertimeout
@defopt vm-pop-server-timeout
@vindex vm-pop-server-timeout
Number of seconds to wait for output from the POP server before
timing out.  It can be set to nil to never time out.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpcrisisautomorph
@deffn Command vm-pcrisis-automorph
@findex vm-pcrisis-automorph
Change contents of the current mail message based on its own headers.
Unless @code{vm-pcrisis-current-state} is @code{no-automorph}, headers and
signatures can be
changed; pre-signatures added; functions called.

Call @code{vm-pcrisis-no-automorph} to disable it for the current buffer.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpcrisisbuildactionstorunlist
@deffn Command vm-pcrisis-build-actions-to-run-list
@findex vm-pcrisis-build-actions-to-run-list
Build a list of the actions to run.
These are the true conditions mapped to actions.  Duplicates will be
eliminated.  You may run it in a composition buffer in order to see what
actions will be run.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpcrisisbuildtrueconditionslist
@deffn Command vm-pcrisis-build-true-conditions-list
@findex vm-pcrisis-build-true-conditions-list
Build list of true conditions and store it in the variable 
@code{vm-pcrisis-true-conditions}.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpcrisischeckconfiguration
@deffn Command vm-pcrisis-check-configuration
@findex vm-pcrisis-check-configuration
Say what is wrong with your Personality Crisis rules, and what to do about it.

Checks that every rule is keyed on a condition that exists and names actions
that exist, that no name is defined twice, that each entry has the form the
variables want, and that a @code{vm-pcrisis-none-true-yet} fallback is last.

The mistake worth the command is a rule keyed on a name no condition has.
Nothing reports it and nothing runs: a fallback rule written as
("default" "from-work") with no condition called "default" leaves the
composition with @code{user-mail-address}, which is the address most people would
have got anyway, so it can go years unnoticed.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpcrisisfixautoprofilesfile
@deffn Command vm-pcrisis-fix-auto-profiles-file
@findex vm-pcrisis-fix-auto-profiles-file
Change @code{vm-pcrisis-auto-profiles-file} to the format used by v0.82+.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpcrisisloadautoprofiles
@deffn Command vm-pcrisis-load-auto-profiles
@findex vm-pcrisis-load-auto-profiles
Initialise @code{vm-pcrisis-auto-profiles} from @code{vm-pcrisis-auto-profiles-file}.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpcrisismigrateprofilestoBBDB
@deffn Command vm-pcrisis-migrate-profiles-to-BBDB
@findex vm-pcrisis-migrate-profiles-to-BBDB
Migrate the profiles stored in @code{vm-pcrisis-auto-profiles-file} to the BBDB.

This will automatically create records if they do not exist and add the new
field @code{vmpc-profile} to the records which is a sexp not meant to be edited.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpcrisismode
@deffn Command vm-pcrisis-mode &optional arg
@findex vm-pcrisis-mode
Personality Crisis: vary the headers and body of a message you send.

Which headers, and how, is decided by @code{vm-pcrisis-conditions} and
@code{vm-pcrisis-actions};
see the commentary at the top of vm-pcrisis.el.

Turning this on advises VM’s composition commands -- replying, forwarding,
resending and starting a new message -- so that the rules are consulted as each
composition begins.  Turning it off removes the advice, leaving those commands
as VM defines them.

This is a global minor mode.  If called interactively, toggle the
@code{Vm-Pcrisis mode} mode.  If the prefix argument is positive, enable the
mode, and if it is zero or negative, disable the mode.

If called from Lisp, toggle the mode if ARG is @code{toggle}.  Enable the
mode if ARG is nil, omitted, or is a positive number.  Disable the mode
if ARG is a negative number.

To check whether the minor mode is enabled in the current buffer,
evaluate @code{(default-value 'vm-pcrisis-mode)}.

The mode’s hook is called both when the mode is enabled and when it is
disabled.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpcrisispromptforprofile
@deffn Command vm-pcrisis-prompt-for-profile &optional remember prompt
@findex vm-pcrisis-prompt-for-profile
Find a profile or prompt for it and add its actions to the list of actions.

A profile is an association between a recipient address and a set of the
actions named in @code{vm-pcrisis-actions}.  When entering the list of actions,
one has
to press ENTER after each action and finish adding action by pressing ENTER
without an action.

The association is stored in @code{vm-pcrisis-auto-profiles-file} and in the
future the
stored actions will automatically run for messages to that address.

REMEMBER can be set to t or @code{prompt}.  When set to @code{prompt} you will be asked
if you want to store the association.  When set to t a new profile will be
stored without asking.

Storing one writes @code{vm-pcrisis-auto-profiles-file}, which is
@code{~/.vmpc-auto-profiles} unless you have said otherwise, and that file is then
consulted by every later composition.  The question names it, and so does the
message after any profile is written or removed, since with REMEMBER t there
is no question to name it.  To be rid of a profile, answer with no actions,
or edit the file.

Set PROMPT to t and you will be prompted each time, i.e. not only for unknown
profiles.  If you want to change the profile only explicitly, then omit the
PROMPT argument and call this function interactively in the composition buffer.
@end deffn

@end macro

