@macro vmdocvmdisplayxfaces
@defopt vm-display-xfaces
@vindex vm-display-xfaces
Non-nil means display images as specified in X-Face headers.
It needs the @code{uncompface} program, named by @code{vm-uncompface-program}, which is
what decodes the header into an image.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmenableshrunkenheaders
@defopt vm-enable-shrunken-headers
@vindex vm-enable-shrunken-headers
Non-nil means fold a header that runs onto more than one line.
A message with fifty recipients puts the subject a page down; with this set
VM shows the first line of such a header and hides the rest behind a widget
you can click, or @code{vm-shrunken-headers-toggle} on the lot.

This needs a presentation buffer, that is @code{vm-always-use-presentation}
non-nil: the overlays it uses would otherwise land in the folder buffer,
which is the file on disk.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmfillparagraphscontaininglonglines
@defopt vm-fill-paragraphs-containing-long-lines
@vindex vm-fill-paragraphs-containing-long-lines
This variable can be set to nil, a numeric value N, the
symbol @code{window-width}.  If it is numeric, it causes VM to fill
paragraphs that contain lines spanning that many columns or more.
Setting it to @code{window-width} has the effect of using the width of
the Emacs window.

Only plain text messages and text/plain MIME parts will be
filled.  The message itself is not modified; its text is copied
into a presentation buffer before the filling is done.

This variable determines which paragraphs are filled,
but @code{vm-paragraph-fill-column} determines the fill column.

Note that filling is carried out only if word wrapping is not in
effect.  The variable @code{vm-word-wrap-paragraphs} controls word
wrapping.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhighlightedheaderregexp
@defopt vm-highlighted-header-regexp
@vindex vm-highlighted-header-regexp
Value specifies which headers to highlight.
This is a regular expression that matches the names of headers that should
be highlighted when a message is first presented.  For example setting
this variable to "From:\\\\|Subject:" causes the From and Subject
headers to be highlighted.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhonorpagedelimiters
@defopt vm-honor-page-delimiters
@vindex vm-honor-page-delimiters
Non-nil value causes VM to honor page delimiters (as specified by the
Emacs page-delimiter variable) when scrolling through a message.
This means that when VM encounters a page delimiter when displaying a
message all the screen lines below that delimiter will be blank until
you scroll past that delimiter.  When you scroll past the delimiter
the text lines between the delimiter and the next delimiter will be
displayed.  Scrolling backward past a page delimiter reverses this
process.

A nil value means ignore page-delimiters.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvminvisibleheaderregexp
@defopt vm-invisible-header-regexp
@vindex vm-invisible-header-regexp
Non-nil value should be a regular expression that tells what headers
VM should NOT normally display when presenting a message.  All other
headers will be displayed.  The variable @code{vm-visible-headers} specifies
the presentation order of headers; headers not matched by
@code{vm-visible-headers} are displayed last.

Nil value causes VM to display ONLY those headers specified in
@code{vm-visible-headers}.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpagecontinuationglyph
@defopt vm-page-continuation-glyph
@vindex vm-page-continuation-glyph
Glyph VM uses to indicate there is more text on the next page.
When VM honors page delimiters (see @code{vm-honor-page-delimiters})
and when VM is previewing a message (see @code{vm-preview-lines}) VM
indicates that there is more text by placing the glyph specified
by this variable at the end of the displayed text.

The value is a string.

Default value: @code{"...press SPACE to see more..."}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmparagraphfillcolumn
@defopt vm-paragraph-fill-column
@vindex vm-paragraph-fill-column
Column beyond which automatic line-wrapping should happen when
re-filling lines longer than the value of
@code{vm-fill-paragraphs-containing-long-lines}.

Default value: @code{70}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpresentationminormodes
@defopt vm-presentation-minor-modes
@vindex vm-presentation-minor-modes
An association list mapping message presentation methods,
such as emacs-w3m, to the corresponding minor modes to be used in
the presentation buffer.

Default value: @code{((emacs-w3m w3m-minor-mode))}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpreviewlines
@defopt vm-preview-lines
@vindex vm-preview-lines
Non-nil value N causes VM to display the visible headers + N lines of text
of a message when it is first presented.  The message is not actually
flagged as read until it is exposed in its entirety.

A value of t causes VM to display as much of the message as will
fit in the window associated with the folder buffer.

A nil value causes VM not to preview messages; no text lines are hidden and
messages are immediately flagged as read.

Default value: @code{0}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpreviewreadmessages
@defopt vm-preview-read-messages
@vindex vm-preview-read-messages
Non-nil value means to preview messages even if they’ve already been read.
A nil value causes VM to preview messages only if new or unread.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmusepresentationminormodes
@defopt vm-use-presentation-minor-modes
@vindex vm-use-presentation-minor-modes
Asks VM to use minor modes for the message presentation text
when appropriate, e.g., when the text is prepared using the
emacs-w3m package.

Customize @code{vm-presentation-minor-modes} to set the appropriate minor modes.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmvisibleheaders
@defopt vm-visible-headers
@vindex vm-visible-headers
List of headers that should be visible when VM first displays a message.
These should be listed in the order you wish them presented.
Regular expressions are allowed.  There’s no need to anchor
patterns with "^", as searches always start at the beginning of
a line.  Put a colon at the end of patterns to get exact matches.
For example, "Date" matches "Date" and "Date-Sent".  Header names
are always matched case insensitively.

If the value of @code{vm-invisible-header-regexp} is nil, only the
headers matched by @code{vm-visible-headers} will be displayed.
Otherwise all headers are displayed except those matched by
@code{vm-invisible-header-regexp}.  In this case @code{vm-visible-headers}
specifies the order in which headers are displayed.  Headers not
matching @code{vm-visible-headers} are displayed last.

Default value:
@example
("Resent-" "From:" "Sender:" "To:" "Newsgroups:" "Apparently-To:"
 "Cc:" "Subject:" "Date:")
@end example
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmwordwrapparagraphs
@defopt vm-word-wrap-paragraphs
@vindex vm-word-wrap-paragraphs
If non-nil, causes VM to word wrap the long lines of a message.
Every line break already there is kept, which is the difference from
vm-fill-paragraphs-containing-long-lines: filling joins the lines of a
paragraph before breaking them again, and so loses where the breaks were.
The column wrapped to is vm-paragraph-fill-column.

Needs nothing installed.  Earlier releases used the longlines library,
obsolete since Emacs 24.4.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcontinuecomposingmessage
@deffn Command vm-continue-composing-message &optional not-picky
@findex vm-continue-composing-message
Find and select the most recently used mail composition buffer.
If the selected buffer is already a Mail mode buffer then it is
buried before beginning the search.  Non Mail mode buffers and
unmodified Mail buffers are skipped.  Prefix arg means unmodified
Mail mode buffers are not skipped.  If no suitable buffer is
found, the current buffer remains selected.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmfilllonglinesinreply
