@macro vmdocvmyankmessage
@deffn Command vm-yank-message message
@findex vm-yank-message
Yank message number N into the current buffer at point.
When called interactively N is always read from the minibuffer.  When
called non-interactively the first argument is expected to be a
message struct.

This command is meant to be used in VM created Mail mode buffers; the
yanked message comes from the mail buffer containing the message you
are replying to, forwarding, or invoked VM’s mail command from.

The whole message is inserted, headers and all, with point left
before it and the mark after it, which is where a function on
@code{mail-citation-hook} looks for what it is to cite.  Anything on
that hook is then run.

With nothing on the hook VM cites the message itself: the yanked
headers are trimmed as specified by @code{vm-included-text-headers} and
@code{vm-included-text-discard-header-regexp}, which between them keep
none by default, and the value of @code{vm-included-text-prefix} is
prepended to every line that is left.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmyankmessageotherfolder
@deffn Command vm-yank-message-other-folder folder
@findex vm-yank-message-other-folder
Like vm-yank-message except the message is yanked from a folder other
than the one that spawned the current Mail mode buffer.  The name of the
folder is read from the minibuffer.

Don’t call this function from a program.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhandlereturnreceipt
@deffn Command vm-handle-return-receipt
@findex vm-handle-return-receipt
Generate a reply to the current message if it requests a return receipt
