See the variable @code{vm-handle-return-receipt-mode} for customization.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailcheckbccremoval
@deffn Command vm-mail-check-bcc-removal
@findex vm-mail-check-bcc-removal
Ask before sending if this composition’s Bcc could reach the other recipients.

A Bcc header is a promise: the addresses in it are told nothing to the people
who receive the message.  With @code{send-mail-function} set to
@code{sendmail-send-it}, VM and Emacs keep the header in the message they hand
to @code{sendmail-program} and trust that program to take it out, because with
-t those addresses are also how it learns whom to deliver to.  When it does
not take it out, everyone on the message reads who was blind copied, and
nothing has failed anywhere that anyone can see (emacs-vm/vm#815).

Asks rather than refuses because a working sendmail, postfix or exim does
remove it, and those are a great many of the people sending mail this way.
Declining says what to change and where it is written down.

@emph{Not autoloaded}: VM has to be loaded before @kbd{M-x} offers this one.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailcheckforemptysubject
@deffn Command vm-mail-check-for-empty-subject
@findex vm-mail-check-for-empty-subject
Check if the subject line is empty and issue an error if so.

@emph{Not autoloaded}: VM has to be loaded before @kbd{M-x} offers this one.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailcheckrecipients
@deffn Command vm-mail-check-recipients
@findex vm-mail-check-recipients
Check if the recipients are specified correctly.
Actually it checks only if there are any missing commas or the like in the
headers.

@emph{Not autoloaded}: VM has to be loaded before @kbd{M-x} offers this one.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailsubjectcleanup
@deffn Command vm-mail-subject-cleanup
@findex vm-mail-subject-cleanup
Do some subject line clean up.
- Replace subject prefixes according to @code{vm-mail-subject-prefix-replacements}.
- Add a number after replies is @code{vm-mail-subject-number-reply} is t.

You might add this function to @code{vm-mail-mode-hook} in order to clean up the
Subject header.

@emph{Not autoloaded}: VM has to be loaded before @kbd{M-x} offers this one.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcheckbccremoval
@defopt vm-check-bcc-removal
@vindex vm-check-bcc-removal
Non-nil means refuse to send a message whose Bcc could reach the recipients.

VM refuses when a composition carries a Bcc header and @code{send-mail-function}
is not one of @code{vm-senders-that-remove-bcc}.  Such a function hands the
header to a program outside Emacs and trusts it to remove it, so the privacy
of the Bcc rests on that program being right.  Where it is not, everyone on
the message learns who was blind copied (emacs-vm/vm#815).

Set this to nil where you know your transport removes it, and VM sends as it
did before.  The check costs nothing when a composition has no Bcc.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcheckforemptysubject
@defopt vm-check-for-empty-subject
@vindex vm-check-for-empty-subject
Non-nil means ask before sending a message with an empty Subject.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcheckrecipients
@defopt vm-check-recipients
@vindex vm-check-recipients
Non-nil means check the recipient headers before sending a message.
A missing comma turns two addresses into one that goes nowhere, which is
what @code{vm-mail-check-recipients} looks for.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcleansubjectprefixes
@defopt vm-clean-subject-prefixes
@vindex vm-clean-subject-prefixes
Non-nil means tidy the Subject prefixes of a new composition.
@code{vm-mail-subject-cleanup} does the work, by
@code{vm-mail-subject-prefix-replacements}.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmcodingsystempriorities
@defopt vm-coding-system-priorities
@vindex vm-coding-system-priorities
List of coding systems for VM to use, for outgoing mail, in order of
preference.

Nil lets Emacs choose.  Set it where Emacs picks a coding system your
recipients cannot read: a list such as @code{(utf-8)}, or ‘(iso-8859-1
iso-8859-15 utf-8)’ to prefer the narrower ones where they will do, puts your
own order on the choice.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmconfirmkillingacomposition
@defopt vm-confirm-killing-a-composition
@vindex vm-confirm-killing-a-composition
Whether VM asks before a composition with writing in it is killed.

A composition buffer belongs to no file, so Emacs kills it without the
question it asks about an unsaved file: @code{kill-buffer} and everything bound to
it, @code{kill-this-buffer} included, took an unsent reply with no warning
(emacs-vm/vm#824).  Nil restores that.

The question is not asked for a composition nothing has been written in, nor
for one that has been sent.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmconfirmmailsend
@defopt vm-confirm-mail-send
@vindex vm-confirm-mail-send
Non-nil means ask before sending a mail message.
This affects @code{vm-mail-send} and @code{vm-mail-send-and-exit} in Mail mode.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmdisablemodesbeforeencoding
@defopt vm-disable-modes-before-encoding
@vindex vm-disable-modes-before-encoding
A list of minor modes to disable before encoding a message.
These modes may slow down (font-lock and *spell) encoding and may
cause trouble (abbrev-mode).

Default value:
@example
(auto-fill-mode font-lock-mode ispell-minor-mode flyspell-mode
		abbrev-mode adaptive-fill-mode)
@end example
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmdndprotocolalist
@defopt vm-dnd-protocol-alist
@vindex vm-dnd-protocol-alist
The functions to call when a drag and drop into a message
composition buffer is done.
See @code{dnd-protocol-alist} for more information.  When nil, behave
as in other buffers.

Default value:
@example
(("^file:///" . vm-dnd-attach-file) ("^file://" . dnd-open-file)
 ("^file:" . vm-dnd-attach-file))
@end example
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmdropbuffernamechars
@defopt vm-drop-buffer-name-chars
@vindex vm-drop-buffer-name-chars
Regexp matching chars to replace by "_" in composition buffer names.
A composition buffer is named after its recipient and subject, and that
name is what the auto-save file is named after, so characters a file
name cannot hold are replaced.  Set to nil to leave names alone.

The default matches control characters and the directory separator.  On
MS-Windows it also matches the rest of the set that platform forbids in
a file name: backslash, colon, asterisk, question mark, double quote,
less-than, greater-than and vertical bar.  That larger set is not used
elsewhere because it includes the colon, which would turn every "Re:"
in a subject into "Re_".

This used to be a US-ASCII whitelist, which replaced every accented
letter as well: a reply to "Ren'e" was named "reply to Ren_".  To
get that back, set this to "[^ a-zA-Z0-9.,_\\"'+-]".

Default value: @code{"[[:cntrl:]/]"}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmfilllonglinesinreplycolumn
@defopt vm-fill-long-lines-in-reply-column
@vindex vm-fill-long-lines-in-reply-column
Fill lines spanning that many columns or more in replies.

Default value: @code{70}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmfillparagraphscontaininglonglinesinreply
@defopt vm-fill-paragraphs-containing-long-lines-in-reply
@vindex vm-fill-paragraphs-containing-long-lines-in-reply
This variable can be set to nil, a numeric value N, the
symbol @code{window-width}.  If it is numeric, it causes VM to fill
included text in replies provided it has lines spanning that many
columns or more.  Setting it to @code{window-width} has the effect of
using the width of the Emacs window.

This variable determines which paragraphs are filled,
but @code{vm-fill-long-lines-in-reply-column} determines the fill column.

Note that filling is carried out only if word wrapping is not in
effect.  The variable @code{vm-word-wrap-paragraphs-in-reply} controls word
wrapping.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmhtmlinreplycolumn
@defopt vm-html-in-reply-column
@vindex vm-html-in-reply-column
Column at which HTML quoted in a reply is broken into lines.
An HTML part carries no line breaks of its own: whatever converts it to
text decides where its lines end, and asked for nothing in particular each
converter uses a width of its own -- emacs-w3m the width of the window the
message happened to be displayed in, lynx 72 columns.  So a reply quoted
lines of one length today and another tomorrow.

A number asks for lines of that width, whatever window the message was read
in; 80, the default, is conventional.  @code{window-width} is the old behaviour,
the width of the window at the time.

There is no setting for text that is not broken at all.  No converter takes
such an instruction, and asking for a page wide enough that no line would
reach the end of it means a page laid out that wide: a centred table, which
is the layout of most HTML mail, then comes back indented by hundreds of
columns.

This governs quoted text only.  Displaying a message still fills to the
window, which is what a window is for.

Default value: @code{80}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvminreplytoformat
@defopt vm-in-reply-to-format
@vindex vm-in-reply-to-format
String which specifies the format of the contents of the In-Reply-To
header that is generated for replies.  See the documentation for the
variable @code{vm-summary-format} for information on what this string may
contain.  The format should *not* end with a newline.
Nil means don’t put an In-Reply-To header in replies.

If the format includes elements with non-ASCII characters, then
"In-Reply-To" should be added to @code{vm-mime-encode-headers-regexp}.

Default value: @code{"%i"}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmincludemimeattachments
@defopt vm-include-mime-attachments
@vindex vm-include-mime-attachments
Non-nil value enables attachments to be included in quoted text in
a reply message.  Otherwise only the button label will be included.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmincludetextbasic
@defopt vm-include-text-basic
@vindex vm-include-text-basic
If true a reply will include the basic text of a message.
This is an old method for citing messages and should not be used
normally.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmincludedmimetypeslist
@defopt vm-included-mime-types-list
@vindex vm-included-mime-types-list
If non-nil, the list of mime type/subtype pairs that should be
included in quoted text in a reply message in addition to the default
types.

This variable currently has an effect only if @code{vm-include-text-basic}
is true.  It has no effect for the default text quotation mechanism
based on MIME decoding.

The default value is nil.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmincludedtextattributionformat
@defopt vm-included-text-attribution-format
@vindex vm-included-text-attribution-format
String which specifies the format of the attribution that precedes the
included text from a message in a reply.  See the documentation for the
variable @code{vm-summary-format} for information on what this string may contain.
Nil means don’t attribute included text in replies.

Default value: @code{"%F writes:\\n"}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmincludedtextdiscardheaderregexp
@defopt vm-included-text-discard-header-regexp
@vindex vm-included-text-discard-header-regexp
Non-nil value should be a regular expression that tells what
headers should not be retained in a message included in a reply.
This variable along with @code{vm-included-text-headers} determines
which headers are retained.

If the value of @code{vm-included-text-discard-header-regexp} is nil,
the headers matched by @code{vm-included-text-headers} are the only headers
that will be retained.

If @code{vm-included-text-discard-header-regexp} is non-nil, then only
headers matched by this variable will not be retained; all
others will be included.  @code{vm-included-text-headers} determines the
header order in that case, with headers not matching any in
the @code{vm-included-text-headers} list appearing last in the header
section of the included text.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmincludedtextheaders
@defopt vm-included-text-headers
@vindex vm-included-text-headers
List of headers that should be retained in a message included in
a reply.  These should be listed in the order you wish them to
appear in the included text.  Regular expressions are allowed.
There’s no need to anchor patterns with "^", as searches always
start at the beginning of a line.  Put a colon at the end of
patterns to get exact matches.  (E.g. "Date" matches "Date"
and "Date-Sent".)  Header names are always matched case
insensitively.

If the value of @code{vm-included-text-discard-header-regexp} is nil,
the headers matched by @code{vm-included-text-headers} are the only
headers that will be retained.

If @code{vm-included-text-discard-header-regexp} is non-nil, then the
headers matched by that variable will be omitted; all the others
will be included.  @code{vm-included-text-headers} determines the
header order in that case, with headers not matching any in the
@code{vm-included-text-headers} list appearing last in the header
section of the included text.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmincludedtextprefix
@defopt vm-included-text-prefix
@vindex vm-included-text-prefix
String used to prefix included text in replies.

Default value: @code{" > "}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmkeepsentmessages
@defopt vm-keep-sent-messages
@vindex vm-keep-sent-messages
Non-nil value N causes VM to keep the last N messages sent from within VM.
@code{Keep} means that VM will not kill the composition buffer after
you send a message with C-c C-c (@code{vm-mail-send-and-exit}).  A
value of 0 or nil causes VM never to keep such buffers.  A value
of t causes VM never to kill such buffers.

Note that these buffers will vanish once you exit Emacs.  To keep a permanent
record of your outgoing mail, use the @code{mail-archive-file-name} variable.

Default value: @code{1}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailautosavedirectory
@defopt vm-mail-auto-save-directory
@vindex vm-mail-auto-save-directory
Directory where messages being composed are auto-saved.  If it is
nil, @code{vm-folder-directory} is used for this purpose.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailcheckrecipientformat
@defopt vm-mail-check-recipient-format
@vindex vm-mail-check-recipient-format
Non-nil value causes @code{vm-mail-send} to check multi-line recipient
headers of outbound mail for lines that don’t end with a
comma.  If such a line is found, an error is signaled and the
mail is not sent.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailheaderfrom
@defopt vm-mail-header-from
@vindex vm-mail-header-from
Non-nil value should be a string that will be appear as the body
of the From header in outbound mail messages.  A nil value means don’t
insert a From header.  This variable also controls the inclusion and
format of the Resent-From header, when resending a message with
@code{vm-resend-message}.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailheaderinsertdate
@defopt vm-mail-header-insert-date
@vindex vm-mail-header-insert-date
Non-nil value causes VM to insert a Date header into a message
when it is sent.  If the message has a Date header, it will be
removed before the new one is inserted.  If the message being
sent is a resent message (i.e. has a Resent- recipient header)
then the Resent-Date header will be removed/inserted instead.

This is useful if you set @code{mail-archive-file-name},
because your archived message will contain a Date header.

A nil value means don’t insert a Date header.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmmailheaderinsertmessageid
@defopt vm-mail-header-insert-message-id
@vindex vm-mail-header-insert-message-id
Non-nil value causes VM to insert a Message-ID header into a message
when it is sent.  If the message has a Message-ID header, it will
be removed before the new one is inserted.  If the message being
sent is a resent message (i.e. has a Resent- recipient header) a
Resent-Message-ID header will be removed/inserted instead.

This is useful if you set @code{mail-archive-file-name}, because your
archived messages will contain a Message-ID header, which may be
useful later for threading messages.

A nil value means don’t insert a Message-ID header.

Default value: @code{t}
@end defopt

@end macro

