@macro vmdocvmopenlineinquotedtext
@defopt vm-open-line-in-quoted-text
@vindex vm-open-line-in-quoted-text
Non-nil means make room when you type inside quoted text.
Typing in the middle of a citation otherwise leaves your words inside the
quotation; with this set VM opens a line for them.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmreplyignoredaddresses
@defopt vm-reply-ignored-addresses
@vindex vm-reply-ignored-addresses
Non-nil value should be a list of regular expressions that match
addresses that VM should automatically remove from the recipient
headers of replies.  These addresses are removed from the headers
before you are placed in the message composition buffer.  So if
you see an address in the header you don’t want you should remove
it yourself.

Case is ignored when matching the addresses.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmreplyignoredreplytos
@defopt vm-reply-ignored-reply-tos
@vindex vm-reply-ignored-reply-tos
Non-nil value should be a list of regular expressions that match
addresses that, if VM finds in a message’s Reply-To header, VM
should ignore the Reply-To header and not use it for replies.  VM
will use the From header instead.

Case is ignored when matching the addresses.

This variable exists solely to provide an escape chute from
mailing lists that add a Reply-To: mailing list header, thereby
leaving no way to reply to just the author of a message.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmreplysubjectprefix
@defopt vm-reply-subject-prefix
@vindex vm-reply-subject-prefix
Non-nil value should be a string that VM should add to the beginning
of the Subject header in replies, if the string is not already present.
Nil means don’t prefix the Subject header.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmstripreplyheaders
@defopt vm-strip-reply-headers
@vindex vm-strip-reply-headers
Non-nil value causes VM to strip away all comments and extraneous text
from the headers generated in reply messages.  If you use the "fakemail"
program as distributed with Emacs, you probably want to set this variable
to t, because as of Emacs v18.52 "fakemail" could not handle unstripped
headers.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmwordwrapparagraphsinreply
@defopt vm-word-wrap-paragraphs-in-reply
@vindex vm-word-wrap-paragraphs-in-reply
If non-nil, causes VM to word wrap the long lines of a composition.
As vm-word-wrap-paragraphs does for a message being read, and for the same
reason: quoted text keeps its own line breaks.  The column wrapped to is
vm-fill-long-lines-in-reply-column.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsmimesignencryptmessage
@deffn Command vm-smime-sign-encrypt-message
@findex vm-smime-sign-encrypt-message
See documentation for @code{vm-smime-sign-message} and
@code{vm-smime-encrypt-message}. This function simply toggles the two
of those and can be used to instruct VM to S/MIME sign and
encrypt an outgoing message upon sending.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmautoarchivemessages
@deffn Command vm-auto-archive-messages &optional prompt
@findex vm-auto-archive-messages
Save all unfiled messages that auto-match a folder via
@code{vm-auto-folder-alist} to their appropriate folders.  Messages that
are flagged for deletion are not saved.  Messages with a "filed"
flag are not saved.

This command asks for confirmation before proceeding.  Set
@code{vm-confirm-for-auto-archive} to nil to turn off the confirmation
dialogue. 

Prefix arg means to prompt user for confirmation for each message
separately. 

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
only marked messages are checked against @code{vm-auto-folder-alist}.  

The saved messages are flagged as @code{filed}.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpipemessagetocommand
@deffn Command vm-pipe-message-to-command command &optional prefixarg discard-output
@findex vm-pipe-message-to-command
Runs a shell command with contents from the current message as input.
By default the headers and the text are used, without the folder’s message
separators.  The prefix argument selects a part instead:

With one C-u the text portion of the message is used.
With two C-u’s the header portion of the message is used.
With three C-u’s the visible header portion of the message
plus the text portion is used.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
each marked message is successively piped to the shell command, one
message per command invocation.  If  applied to collapsed threads in 
summary and thread operations are enabled via
@code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the thread are piped. 

Output, if any, is displayed.  The message is not altered.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpipemessagetocommanddiscardoutput
@deffn Command vm-pipe-message-to-command-discard-output command &optional prefixarg
@findex vm-pipe-message-to-command-discard-output
Run a shell command with contents from the current message as input.
This function is like @code{vm-pipe-message-to-command}, but will not display the
output of the command.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpipemessagestocommand
@deffn Command vm-pipe-messages-to-command command &optional prefixarg discard-output no-wait
@findex vm-pipe-messages-to-command
Run a shell command with contents from messages as input.

Similar to @code{vm-pipe-message-to-command}, but it will call process
just once and pipe all messages to it.  For bulk operations this
is much faster than calling the command on each message.  This is
more like saving to a pipe.

With one C-u the text portion of the messages is used.
With two C-u’s the header portion of the messages is used.
With three C-u’s the visible header portion of the messages
plus the text portion is used.

Leading and trailing separators are included with each message
depending on the settings of @code{vm-pipe-messages-to-command-start}
and @code{vm-pipe-messages-to-command-end}.

Output, if any, is displayed unless DISCARD-OUTPUT is t.

If NO-WAIT is t, then do not wait for process to finish, if it is
a function then call it with the COMMAND and OUTPUT-BUFFER as
arguments after the command finished.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpipemessagestocommanddiscardoutput
@deffn Command vm-pipe-messages-to-command-discard-output command &optional prefixarg
@findex vm-pipe-messages-to-command-discard-output
Runs a shell command with contents from the current message as input.
This function is like @code{vm-pipe-messages-to-command}, but will not display the
output of the command.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmpipemessagestocommandtostring
@deffn Command vm-pipe-messages-to-command-to-string command &optional prefixarg
@findex vm-pipe-messages-to-command-to-string
Runs a shell command with contents from the current message as input.
This function is like @code{vm-pipe-messages-to-command}, but will not display the
output of the command, but return it as a string.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmprintmessage
@deffn Command vm-print-message &optional count
@findex vm-print-message
Print the current message
Prefix arg N means print the current message and the next N - 1 messages.
Prefix arg -N means print the current message and the previous N - 1 messages.

The variable @code{vm-print-command} controls what command is run to
print the message, and @code{vm-print-command-switches} is a list of switches
to pass to the command.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
each marked message is printed, one message per vm-print-command
invocation.  If applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread
operations are enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages
in the thread are printed.

Output, if any, is displayed.  The message is not altered.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsavemessage
@deffn Command vm-save-message folder &optional count mlist quiet
@findex vm-save-message
Save the current message to another FOLDER, queried via the
mini-buffer.  The FOLDER may be a local file system folder or an
IMAP folder.  You can specify a preference by setting the
variable @code{vm-imap-save-to-server}.

Prefix arg COUNT means save this message and the next COUNT-1
messages.  A negative COUNT means save this message and the
previous COUNT-1 messages.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),       
all marked messages in the current folder are saved; other messages are
ignored.  If applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread operations are
enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages in the
thread are saved.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsavemessagesansheaders
@deffn Command vm-save-message-sans-headers file &optional count quiet
@findex vm-save-message-sans-headers
Save the current message to a file, without its header section.
If the file already exists, the message body will be appended to it.
Prefix arg COUNT means save the next COUNT message bodies.  A
negative COUNT means save the previous COUNT bodies.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
only the next COUNT marked messages are saved; other intervening
messages are ignored.  If applied to collapsed threads in summary and
thread operations are enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all
messages in the thread are saved.

The saved messages are flagged as @code{written}.

This command should NOT be used to save message to mail folders; use
@code{vm-save-message} instead (normally bound to @code{s}).
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsavemessagetoimapfolder
@deffn Command vm-save-message-to-imap-folder folder &optional count mlist _quiet
@findex vm-save-message-to-imap-folder
Save the current message to an IMAP folder.
Prefix arg COUNT means save this message and the next COUNT-1
messages.  A negative COUNT means save this message and the
previous COUNT-1 messages.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
all marked messages in the current folder are saved; other messages are
ignored.  If applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread
operations are enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all
messages in the thread are saved.

The saved messages are flagged as @code{filed}.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmsavemessagetolocalfolder
@deffn Command vm-save-message-to-local-folder folder &optional count mlist quiet
@findex vm-save-message-to-local-folder
Save the current message to a mail folder.
If the folder already exists, the message will be appended to it.

Prefix arg COUNT means save this message and the next COUNT-1
messages.  A negative COUNT means save this message and the
previous COUNT-1 messages.

When invoked on marked messages (via @code{vm-next-command-uses-marks}),
all marked messages in the current folder are saved; other messages are
ignored.  If  applied to collapsed threads in summary and thread
operations are enabled via @code{vm-enable-thread-operations} then all messages
in the thread are saved.

The saved messages are flagged as @code{filed}.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmisearchbackward
@deffn Command vm-isearch-backward &optional arg
@findex vm-isearch-backward
Incrementally search backward through the current folder’s messages.
Usage is identical to the standard Emacs incremental search.
When the search terminates the message containing point will be selected.

If the variable vm-search-using-regexps is non-nil, regular expressions
are understood; nil means the search will be for the input string taken
literally.  Specifying a prefix ARG interactively toggles the value of
vm-search-using-regexps for this search.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmisearchforward
@deffn Command vm-isearch-forward &optional arg
@findex vm-isearch-forward
Incrementally search forward through the current folder’s messages.
Usage is identical to the standard Emacs incremental search.
When the search terminates the message containing point will be selected.

If the variable vm-search-using-regexps is non-nil, regular expressions
are understood; nil means the search will be for the input string taken
literally.  Specifying a prefix ARG interactively toggles the value of
vm-search-using-regexps for this search.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmgrepmail
@deffn Command vm-grepmail arguments folders
@findex vm-grepmail
A not so excellent interface to grepmail.
Grepmail is a fast perl-script for finding mails which got lost in the
folder jungle.  End your input or folders and directories with an empty sting
or the default folder.

ARGUMENTS the command line arguments to grepmail.
FOLDERS should be a list of files/directories to search in.
@end deffn

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmgrepmailarguments
@defopt vm-grepmail-arguments
@vindex vm-grepmail-arguments
Arguments for grepmail program.

Default value: worked out when VM is loaded, from this system.
@end defopt

@end macro

@macro vmdocvmgrepmailcommand
@defopt vm-grepmail-command
@vindex vm-grepmail-command
Path to the program.

